00:06:41.016 --> 00:06:44.967
The Open Web Application security
project, or OWASP, is a nonprofit

00:06:44.967 --> 00:06:48.027
foundation that was created to
help improve application security.

00:06:48.477 --> 00:06:52.317
The OWASP Foundation launched on
December 1st, 2001, and is most well

00:06:52.317 --> 00:06:56.757
known for its Top 10 list open source
tools and other security projects.

00:06:57.147 --> 00:07:02.007
The OWASP API Security Top 10 is a list
of the most critical security risks

00:07:02.007 --> 00:07:03.837
for application programming interfaces.

00:07:04.302 --> 00:07:08.392
The founders of the project
include Erez Yalon and Inon Shkedy.

00:07:08.742 --> 00:07:12.972
The OWASP API Security Top 10 was
originally released in December, 2019

00:07:12.977 --> 00:07:15.342
and was driven by several key factors.

00:07:15.612 --> 00:07:18.342
The first was the rapid rise of APIs.

00:07:18.792 --> 00:07:23.652
APIs power the flow of the world's
most valuable resource, which is data.

00:07:24.162 --> 00:07:28.482
A business no longer needs to specialize
in all aspects of creating software.

00:07:28.662 --> 00:07:31.812
Instead, they can use the features
of software shared by other

00:07:31.812 --> 00:07:33.162
companies and organizations.

00:07:33.822 --> 00:07:37.632
Historically, the issue with doing
this was the disconnected nature of

00:07:37.632 --> 00:07:41.352
different programming languages, web
application programming interfaces

00:07:41.352 --> 00:07:45.522
allowed for a common method to consume
or provide data across the internet.

00:07:46.182 --> 00:07:49.572
Since the widespread adoption of
web APIs, organizations have been

00:07:49.577 --> 00:07:51.402
enabled to leverage the functionality.

00:07:51.552 --> 00:07:52.872
Of other applications.

00:07:53.112 --> 00:07:56.562
Instead of having to develop
custom software for maps, gps,

00:07:56.562 --> 00:08:00.102
payment processing, authentication,
communication, and much more,

00:08:00.552 --> 00:08:04.452
developers can leverage APIs to use
the functionality of other applications

00:08:04.452 --> 00:08:06.132
that specialize in that given area.

00:08:06.672 --> 00:08:11.622
APIs are a major business enabler, which
explains the global rapid adoption.

00:08:12.402 --> 00:08:15.012
Second, a major gap in security.

00:08:15.012 --> 00:08:18.502
So the next factor that compounded
the effects of the other two is the

00:08:18.502 --> 00:08:21.892
fact that the tools and techniques
of the past were not effective at

00:08:21.892 --> 00:08:24.112
detecting API related vulnerabilities.

00:08:24.532 --> 00:08:27.772
The tools and techniques that were used
for enterprise vulnerability management

00:08:27.777 --> 00:08:31.792
programs, web applications, scanners, and
traditional network security monitoring

00:08:31.792 --> 00:08:35.782
tools were not designed to handle
the unique challenges posed by APIs.

00:08:36.322 --> 00:08:39.772
As a result, many organizations
were not adequately prepared to

00:08:39.772 --> 00:08:43.372
defend against API attacks leaving
them vulnerable to data breaches.

00:08:44.362 --> 00:08:46.612
Third, a new leading attack vector.

00:08:46.972 --> 00:08:49.822
Often when it comes to the rapid
adoption of new technologies,

00:08:49.822 --> 00:08:51.352
security is an afterthought.

00:08:51.862 --> 00:08:53.182
APIs are no different.

00:08:53.302 --> 00:08:57.082
The rapid adoption of APIs led to
a new attack vector that exposes

00:08:57.082 --> 00:08:58.713
data application functionality.

00:08:59.503 --> 00:09:03.373
Public internet facing APIs often
bypassed all of the security

00:09:03.373 --> 00:09:06.553
measures that had grown with
businesses over the past decades.

00:09:06.703 --> 00:09:10.213
An attacker no longer needs to go
through the classic MITRE cyber kill

00:09:10.213 --> 00:09:14.383
chain by bypassing the firewall, gaining
entry to the network, pivoting around

00:09:14.383 --> 00:09:18.373
to system that contains data, and then
finding a way to exfiltrate that data.

00:09:18.823 --> 00:09:22.513
Instead, an attacker can use
an insecure API and have direct

00:09:22.513 --> 00:09:24.193
access to that sensitive data.

00:09:24.718 --> 00:09:29.128
In response to the massive adoption of
APIs, the security gaps introduced by API

00:09:29.128 --> 00:09:34.678
providers, and the new wave of API related
security incidents, the OWASP API Security

00:09:34.683 --> 00:09:36.738
Project published its Top 10 list.

00:09:37.258 --> 00:09:41.548
The API-focused Top 10 list provides
a comprehensive set of guidelines,

00:09:41.548 --> 00:09:45.658
best practices, and tools to help
organizations secure their APIs and

00:09:45.658 --> 00:09:47.788
protect against API related threats.

00:09:49.248 --> 00:09:53.208
The OWASP API Security Project has
become a widely recognized and respected

00:09:53.208 --> 00:09:57.198
resource for security professionals
and has helped raise awareness of

00:09:57.203 --> 00:09:59.058
the importance of API security.

00:10:01.481 --> 00:10:02.981
How is the top 10 compiled?

00:10:03.281 --> 00:10:08.201
As described by Paulo Silva, OWASP
API Security Project leader, the 2023

00:10:08.201 --> 00:10:12.941
OWASP API Security Top 10 list is put
together by the project team based on

00:10:13.331 --> 00:10:18.236
internal research, based on publicly
available data, bounty platforms and news.

00:10:18.566 --> 00:10:21.836
Since the project did not receive
any data during the call for data

00:10:21.836 --> 00:10:26.396
during the two month window in late
2022, the top 10 list was compiled

00:10:26.396 --> 00:10:28.286
based on the project team's research.

00:10:28.616 --> 00:10:32.456
This was not something new or different
for the project as this is, as

00:10:32.461 --> 00:10:34.886
Silva stated, what was done in 2019.

00:10:35.626 --> 00:10:39.406
Without dataset received from
the community, the OWASP project

00:10:39.406 --> 00:10:43.276
team had to compile data based on
public disclosures from bug bounty

00:10:43.276 --> 00:10:47.566
pro platforms and API security
incidents that made it into the news.

00:10:47.836 --> 00:10:52.276
Although this dataset isn't as ideal
as data compiled directly from testing

00:10:52.276 --> 00:10:55.966
hundreds of thousands of endpoints
across the internet, it does represent

00:10:55.966 --> 00:10:58.516
a sample of real-world API weaknesses.

00:10:58.871 --> 00:11:03.111
Some potential  issues with applying
this data include bug bounty

00:11:03.116 --> 00:11:06.881
program  incentivizes certain
types of findings over others.

00:11:07.241 --> 00:11:11.141
Bug bounty programs attract participants
that represent a small sample of the

00:11:11.141 --> 00:11:12.611
APIs that are actually out in the wild.

00:11:13.261 --> 00:11:17.381
Newsworthy incidents often leave
security researchers without

00:11:17.381 --> 00:11:18.491
specific technical details.

00:11:19.296 --> 00:11:22.686
Obviously newsworthy incidents do
not include all of the breaches

00:11:22.686 --> 00:11:25.896
and security incidents that
are not reported or publicized.

00:11:26.286 --> 00:11:30.156
That being the case, there are lessons
to be learned and risks to be aware

00:11:30.161 --> 00:11:34.176
of based on bug bounty, writeups,
and publicized security incidents.

00:11:34.656 --> 00:11:37.866
If you are interested in learning
more about the sources, belows a

00:11:37.866 --> 00:11:41.646
list of common websites to dive
deeper into bug bounty research.

00:11:42.506 --> 00:11:46.226
In addition to these, attached
to this module is a JSON extract

00:11:46.226 --> 00:11:49.976
from PentesterLand that contains
all documented API writeups.

00:11:50.336 --> 00:11:54.936
PentesterLand writeup compilation,
HackerOne hack activity, Awesome

00:11:54.936 --> 00:11:58.023
Bugbounty writeups, all the
links are in the content below.

00:11:58.613 --> 00:12:02.213
Also, in the course content is a list
of some of the most newsworthy API

00:12:02.513 --> 00:12:06.083
incidents in the last five years,
including data breaches and leaks from

00:12:06.083 --> 00:12:09.743
the USPS, Optus, T-Mobile, and Toyota.

00:12:10.348 --> 00:12:14.188
The OWASP API Security Top 10 list
contains common groups of the most

00:12:14.188 --> 00:12:18.868
critical security risks to be on the
lookout for based on these sources.

00:12:22.087 --> 00:12:24.877
The OWASP API security
risks are associated with

00:12:24.877 --> 00:12:26.767
references to external sources.

00:12:27.187 --> 00:12:33.097
These sources include CWEs, or Common
Weakness Enumeration, other OAS projects,

00:12:33.307 --> 00:12:37.297
and the National Institute of Standards
and Technology, or NIST Guidance.

00:12:38.407 --> 00:12:40.777
Most of the references involve CWE.

00:12:40.997 --> 00:12:44.767
CWEs are a list of common software
and hardware vulnerabilities developed

00:12:44.767 --> 00:12:46.717
by the community and hosted by MITRE.

00:12:47.317 --> 00:12:51.887
Each CWE is identified by a
unique identifier or CWEID.

00:12:52.507 --> 00:12:55.777
This identifier can be used to refer
back to a specific vulnerability.

00:12:56.437 --> 00:12:59.857
Check out the full table of external
sources in the course material below.

00:13:00.697 --> 00:13:04.147
Understanding the external sources and
how they're associated with a given

00:13:04.147 --> 00:13:08.497
OWASP API Security Top 10 risk will help
provide you with additional insight.

00:13:09.037 --> 00:13:11.857
Throughout the course external
sources will be provided for the

00:13:11.857 --> 00:13:13.867
OWASP API Security Top 10 risks.

00:13:15.052 --> 00:13:19.162
These resources will be provided to you
to help you dive in as deep as you'd

00:13:19.162 --> 00:13:21.322
like into the topic of API security.

00:13:21.862 --> 00:13:24.447
We will dive deeper into
these  in later modules.

00:13:24.807 --> 00:13:27.837
In the next section, we'll go
over the updates to the top

00:13:27.837 --> 00:13:30.237
10 list from 2019 to 2023.

00:13:32.943 --> 00:13:35.553
Since the release of the
OWASP API Security Top 10 in

00:13:35.553 --> 00:13:38.073
2019, API usage has grown.

00:13:38.433 --> 00:13:43.203
API related data breaches have continued
and new API technologies have emerged.

00:13:43.593 --> 00:13:46.503
All of this has played a role in the
need for an updated version of the

00:13:46.503 --> 00:13:48.693
API security project's Top 10 list.

00:13:49.023 --> 00:13:53.403
Since the 2019 Top 10, several events
have demonstrated the importance of

00:13:53.403 --> 00:13:55.533
keeping the list updated and relevant.

00:13:56.253 --> 00:13:58.473
API attacks are consistently on the rise.

00:13:58.863 --> 00:14:05.793
Akamai reported seeing nearly 114 million
API attacks in a single day in 2021.

00:14:06.273 --> 00:14:11.613
The global API market value was
valued at 2.2 billion in 2021, and

00:14:11.613 --> 00:14:15.363
was predicted to reach 41.5 by 2031.

00:14:15.603 --> 00:14:18.213
That's a 20 times growth
rate, in 10 years.

00:14:18.663 --> 00:14:23.883
In 2022, Postman had over 46 million
Postman collections, and GitHub

00:14:23.883 --> 00:14:26.473
had 3 million API related repos.

00:14:27.388 --> 00:14:32.338
APIs continue to be rapidly adopted and
the financial stakes continue to climb.

00:14:32.758 --> 00:14:36.658
The OWASP API Security Project updated
the Top 10 list to address some of the

00:14:36.658 --> 00:14:40.288
changes in the attack trends that have
emerged since the previous version.

00:14:40.808 --> 00:14:44.488
Inon Shkedy, OWASP API Security
Project Leader, stated that the

00:14:44.488 --> 00:14:49.048
OWASP API Top 10 2023 version is
different from the 2019 version.

00:14:49.438 --> 00:14:53.278
"We aspire to follow the security
trends that are relevant to APIs and

00:14:53.278 --> 00:14:55.108
have been developed in recent years.

00:14:55.703 --> 00:14:58.613
If you try to hack or protect
an API that was developed five

00:14:58.613 --> 00:15:02.183
years ago, it would make more
sense to refer to the 2019 list."

00:15:03.144 --> 00:15:08.274
At a high level, when you compare the Top
10 from 2019 to 2023, you see that two

00:15:08.274 --> 00:15:13.284
previous risks were removed from the list,
three remained exactly the same, four were

00:15:13.284 --> 00:15:16.044
updated, and five of the 2023 are new.

00:15:16.839 --> 00:15:20.649
The two items that were removed
included Injection and Insufficient

00:15:20.649 --> 00:15:21.639
Logging and Monitoring.

00:15:22.209 --> 00:15:25.299
These two risks were not completely
mitigated and resolved, but the

00:15:25.299 --> 00:15:28.509
importance of new emerging risks
pushed them beyond the Top 10.

00:15:29.199 --> 00:15:32.859
Logging and Monitoring is a classic
OWASP Top 10 risk, but it is not

00:15:32.864 --> 00:15:36.549
an item that is represented in
breaches or disclosed bug bounties.

00:15:37.164 --> 00:15:41.634
Injection attacks are still around and
many web APIs are still susceptible,

00:15:41.634 --> 00:15:45.174
but the adoption of web application
firewalls and implementation of other

00:15:45.179 --> 00:15:49.464
mitigation techniques has reduced the
occurrence of notable API injection.

00:15:50.634 --> 00:15:53.004
There were three categories
that remained on the list.

00:15:53.124 --> 00:15:57.864
Broken Object Level Authorization or
BOLA, Broken Function Level Authorization,

00:15:57.864 --> 00:15:59.934
or BFLA, and Security Misconfiguration.

00:16:01.209 --> 00:16:06.009
BOLA and BFLA are still among the most
common API risks and account for many of

00:16:06.009 --> 00:16:07.779
the breaches in bug bounty disclosures.

00:16:08.259 --> 00:16:11.859
APIs continue to have severe issues
when it comes to authorization risk.

00:16:12.549 --> 00:16:16.179
The Security Misconfiguration category
contains a wide range of possible

00:16:16.179 --> 00:16:18.579
misconfigurations that can plague APIs.

00:16:18.969 --> 00:16:22.509
In other words, this category is unlikely
to leave the Top 10 because it is a

00:16:22.509 --> 00:16:24.669
catchall for many API vulnerabilities.

00:16:25.659 --> 00:16:27.789
Four categories were renamed.

00:16:27.879 --> 00:16:31.629
The renaming of these categories helped
simplify the risk categories and bring

00:16:31.629 --> 00:16:33.369
additional focus to the issue at hand.

00:16:33.999 --> 00:16:37.359
Broken User Authentication has
now become Broken Authentication.

00:16:37.689 --> 00:16:40.509
This simplification has done
away with User and now brings

00:16:40.509 --> 00:16:43.809
additional attention to the API
authentication as a whole process.

00:16:44.529 --> 00:16:48.099
Improper Assets Management has
become Improper Inventory Management.

00:16:48.549 --> 00:16:52.179
This change was likely made to better
align with industry terminology and

00:16:52.179 --> 00:16:54.129
to cover a wider range of resources.

00:16:54.459 --> 00:16:58.659
The term Assets might only refer to
tangible items valued by an organization

00:16:58.659 --> 00:17:02.229
while the term Inventory could
refer to a wider set of resources.

00:17:02.799 --> 00:17:06.639
There were a total of five categories that
were added to the OWASP API Security Top

00:17:06.639 --> 00:17:09.909
10 2023 that were not on the 2019 version.

00:17:10.614 --> 00:17:15.084
Server Side Request Forgery and Unsafe
Consumption of APIs are completely new and

00:17:15.084 --> 00:17:20.004
did not have previous representation on
the 2019 OWASP API Security Top 10 list.

00:17:20.564 --> 00:17:24.504
Server Side Request Forgery was
represented on the 2021 OWASP Top

00:17:24.504 --> 00:17:28.404
10 list and was added to that list
based on survey results rather

00:17:28.404 --> 00:17:29.964
than by incident occurrence.

00:17:30.639 --> 00:17:34.359
The remaining new risk categories
included Broken Object Property Level

00:17:34.359 --> 00:17:39.159
Authorization, or BOPLA, Unrestricted
Resource Consumption, and Unrestricted

00:17:39.164 --> 00:17:43.929
Access to Sensitive Business Flows, each
containing elements from the 2019 list.

00:17:44.349 --> 00:17:48.189
For example, BOPLA is a combination
of Excessive Data Exposure and Mass

00:17:48.189 --> 00:17:51.909
Assignment, and we will dive deeper
into each of these later in the course.

00:17:52.318 --> 00:17:56.338
Paired with each OWASP API security
risk category are Risk Ratings.

00:17:56.848 --> 00:18:00.388
The risk ratings were based on
the OWASP risk rating methodology.

00:18:00.748 --> 00:18:04.078
The project now refers to the
OWASP risk rating framework.

00:18:04.408 --> 00:18:08.248
In the written course content, there is a
table demonstrating the risk ratings used

00:18:08.248 --> 00:18:13.828
for the OWASP API security project from
the OWASP API Security Top 10 2019 pdf.

00:18:14.608 --> 00:18:18.538
Any of these factors could significantly
affect the overall likelihood of

00:18:18.538 --> 00:18:21.508
an attacker finding and exploiting
a particular vulnerability.

00:18:22.078 --> 00:18:26.698
This rating does not take into account
the actual impact on a specific business.

00:18:27.388 --> 00:18:30.658
The risk score must be determined
by the given organization,

00:18:30.658 --> 00:18:32.248
as the project team states.

00:18:32.458 --> 00:18:36.028
The purpose of the OWASP API
Security Top 10 is not to do

00:18:36.028 --> 00:18:37.588
this risk analysis for you.

00:18:37.948 --> 00:18:40.828
In other words, the purpose of
the OWASP API Security Top 10 was

00:18:40.833 --> 00:18:44.488
not to perform a specific risk
analysis for a given organization,

00:18:44.848 --> 00:18:48.598
but to provide a guideline for
organizations to consider risk factors.

00:18:49.363 --> 00:18:52.393
In the course content, you can see
a table containing the changes to

00:18:52.393 --> 00:18:54.823
the 2019 and 2023 risk ratings.

00:18:58.807 --> 00:19:01.987
The classic equation for risk
is likelihood multiplied by

00:19:01.987 --> 00:19:04.207
impact with each risk score.

00:19:04.207 --> 00:19:08.227
The OWASP API Security Project team
has left the impact as a relative

00:19:08.227 --> 00:19:10.597
value to be determined by the business.

00:19:10.957 --> 00:19:14.347
In addition, the project team
includes a note along with these

00:19:14.352 --> 00:19:17.587
scores that the likelihood of these
scores is not taken into account.

00:19:18.362 --> 00:19:22.802
What we are left with are values that
represent overall exploitability,

00:19:23.132 --> 00:19:28.032
including complexity, exploitability,
weakness prevalence, weakness

00:19:28.122 --> 00:19:30.402
detectability, and technical impact.

00:19:30.822 --> 00:19:34.932
Note that the overall score does not
equate to a risk score because significant

00:19:34.932 --> 00:19:38.232
factors such as business impact and
likelihood are not accounted for.

00:19:41.372 --> 00:19:45.872
API1:2023 Broken Object Level
Authorization, or BOLA, is

00:19:45.872 --> 00:19:49.202
one of the most prevalent and
severe vulnerabilities for APIs.

00:19:49.572 --> 00:19:53.522
BOLA vulnerabilities occur when an API
provider does not have sufficient controls

00:19:53.522 --> 00:19:55.202
in place to enforce authorization.

00:19:55.712 --> 00:19:59.162
In other words, API users should
only have access to sensitive

00:19:59.162 --> 00:20:00.632
resources that belong to them.

00:20:01.052 --> 00:20:04.202
When BOLA is present, an attacker
will be able to access the

00:20:04.202 --> 00:20:05.702
sensitive data of other users.

00:20:06.587 --> 00:20:09.017
The OWASP attack vector
description states:

00:20:09.197 --> 00:20:13.667
"Attackers can exploit API endpoints that
are vulnerable to a broken object level

00:20:13.667 --> 00:20:18.647
authorization by manipulating the ID of
an object that is sent within the request.

00:20:18.917 --> 00:20:24.807
Object IDs can be anything from sequential
integers, UUIDs or generic strings.

00:20:25.302 --> 00:20:29.412
Regardless of the data type, they're
easy to identify in the request target,

00:20:29.832 --> 00:20:34.272
either through the path or a query
string parameter request headers, or

00:20:34.272 --> 00:20:36.852
even as a part of the request payload."

00:20:37.527 --> 00:20:40.347
The OWASP security weakness
description states:

00:20:40.557 --> 00:20:44.277
"This has been the most common
and impactful attack on APIs.

00:20:44.547 --> 00:20:48.387
Authorization and access control
mechanisms in modern web applications

00:20:48.387 --> 00:20:49.947
are complex and widespread.

00:20:50.457 --> 00:20:53.847
Even if the application implements
a proper infrastructure for

00:20:53.847 --> 00:20:57.897
authorization checks, developers
might forget to use these checks

00:20:57.897 --> 00:20:59.997
before accessing a sensitive object.

00:21:00.417 --> 00:21:04.407
Access controlled detection is not
typically amenable to automated

00:21:04.407 --> 00:21:06.177
static or dynamic testing."

00:21:06.752 --> 00:21:09.122
The OWASP impact description states:

00:21:09.272 --> 00:21:13.742
"Unauthorized access can result in data
disclosure to unauthorized parties,

00:21:13.772 --> 00:21:15.962
data loss or data manipulation.

00:21:16.352 --> 00:21:20.402
Unauthorized access to objects can
also lead to full account takeover."

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:25.450
If an API endpoint has insufficient
access controls, it will not

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:29.620
perform checks to ensure users can
only access their own resources.

00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:33.460
When these controls are missing,
User A will be able to obtain User

00:21:33.460 --> 00:21:35.890
B's resources via API requests.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:40.660
APIs use some sort of value like names
or numbers to identify various objects.

00:21:41.005 --> 00:21:45.445
When an attacker discovers an API's
resource ID, they will attempt to obtain

00:21:45.445 --> 00:21:49.825
the resources when unauthenticated
or authenticated as a different user.

00:21:50.395 --> 00:21:53.875
For instance, imagine that
an authenticated user Bruce

00:21:53.995 --> 00:22:03.055
sends a GET request to
https://herohospital.com/api/v3/users?id=2727

00:22:03.145 --> 00:22:07.885
and receives a JSON response back
with his data, this poses no problem.

00:22:08.170 --> 00:22:11.050
Bruce should be able to access
Bruce's own information.

00:22:11.530 --> 00:22:15.460
However, if Bruce is able to access
another user's information, then a

00:22:15.460 --> 00:22:17.170
BOLA vulnerability would be present.

00:22:17.830 --> 00:22:22.630
This weakness can be tested by using
other resource IDs in place of 2727.

00:22:23.200 --> 00:22:25.780
Say Bruce is able to obtain
information about another

00:22:25.780 --> 00:22:33.380
user by sending a request to:
https://herohospital.com/api/v3/users?id=2728,

00:22:33.655 --> 00:22:38.305
and receives a response about Harvey
Dent and his dissociative identity

00:22:38.305 --> 00:22:40.555
disorder, then there would be a problem.

00:22:41.005 --> 00:22:44.275
Assuming that Bruce is still using
his authorization to access this

00:22:44.275 --> 00:22:48.595
data, this would be a clear indication
that the API is vulnerable to BOLA.

00:22:49.295 --> 00:22:53.185
BOLA isn't always as simple as this
example because there's flexibility

00:22:53.185 --> 00:22:57.235
in how resources are provisioned and
requested from one API to the next.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:01.950
The written course content contains
several examples of how API resources

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.630
may be requested and attacked.

00:23:04.200 --> 00:23:08.130
In these examples, tests can be performed
by replacing the bolded resource

00:23:08.130 --> 00:23:10.140
IDs with other numbers or words.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:14.770
Of course, in POST request, the resource
could also be requested in the POST body.

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:19.880
If User A can successfully access
the information of any other user,

00:23:20.150 --> 00:23:21.830
then there's a vulnerability present.

00:23:22.220 --> 00:23:25.280
BOLA vulnerabilities are the
most common API vulnerabilities

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:29.330
and are easily exploitable and
require a low amount of technical

00:23:29.335 --> 00:23:31.130
skills in order to discover these.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:36.380
BOLA can present itself in many forms
depending on how resources are organized.

00:23:36.710 --> 00:23:39.560
As you can see in the written
course, content resources can be

00:23:39.560 --> 00:23:43.850
organized by a user, by group,
or by a combination of both.

00:23:44.300 --> 00:23:48.650
In all of these instances, only those
with the proper permission should be

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.720
allowed to retrieve their resources.

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:54.020
The OWASP preventative measures state:

00:23:54.230 --> 00:23:57.530
In order to improve API security,
it is important to implement

00:23:57.530 --> 00:23:59.360
robust authorization controls.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:03.140
These controls should consider
user policies and role-based

00:24:03.140 --> 00:24:04.640
access control hierarchies.

00:24:05.120 --> 00:24:08.750
The primary focus should be to
ensure that authenticated users

00:24:08.750 --> 00:24:12.410
only have access to resources they
are authorized to have access to.

00:24:13.040 --> 00:24:16.220
Using less predictable resource
IDs can increase the challenge

00:24:16.220 --> 00:24:19.730
of a user or attacker guessing
the resource ID of other users.

00:24:20.190 --> 00:24:22.980
Developers should perform
tests that specifically test

00:24:22.980 --> 00:24:24.570
for authorization controls.

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:28.530
Implement a proper authorization
mechanism that relies on the

00:24:28.530 --> 00:24:30.210
user policies and hierarchy.

00:24:30.930 --> 00:24:35.640
Use the authorization mechanism to
check if the logged in user has access

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:39.720
to perform the requested action on
the record in every function that

00:24:39.720 --> 00:24:43.530
uses an input from the client to
access the record in the database.

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:50.210
Prefer the use of random and unpredictable
values, such as GUIDS for records IDs.

00:24:50.750 --> 00:24:54.530
Write tests to evaluate the vulnerability
of the authorization mechanism.

00:24:54.860 --> 00:24:57.860
Do not deploy changes
that make the tests fail.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:01.880
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about BOLA.

00:25:09.986 --> 00:25:13.936
API2:2023, Broken Authentication,
refers to any weakness within

00:25:13.936 --> 00:25:15.706
the API authentication process.

00:25:16.216 --> 00:25:19.096
All APIs that contain sensitive
information should have some

00:25:19.096 --> 00:25:20.866
mechanism to authenticate users.

00:25:21.286 --> 00:25:25.516
Authentication is the process that is used
to verify the identity of an API user.

00:25:25.846 --> 00:25:28.606
Whether that is a person,
device, or system.

00:25:29.356 --> 00:25:33.286
In other words, authentication is the
process of verifying that an entity

00:25:33.286 --> 00:25:35.176
is who that entity claims to be.

00:25:35.596 --> 00:25:39.256
This verification process is normally
done with the use of a username

00:25:39.256 --> 00:25:43.966
and password combination, a token
and or multifactor authentication.

00:25:45.241 --> 00:25:48.901
Authentication related vulnerabilities
typically occur when an API provider

00:25:48.901 --> 00:25:52.981
either doesn't implement a strong
authentication mechanism or implements

00:25:52.981 --> 00:25:54.901
an authentication process incorrectly.

00:25:56.041 --> 00:26:00.571
The OWASP Attack Vector description for
this states that "The authentication

00:26:00.571 --> 00:26:04.946
mechanism is an easy target for
attackers since it's exposed to everyone.

00:26:05.426 --> 00:26:09.056
Although more advanced technical
skills may be required to exploit some

00:26:09.056 --> 00:26:13.046
authentication issues, exploitation
tools are generally available."

00:26:14.036 --> 00:26:17.906
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states "Software and security

00:26:17.906 --> 00:26:21.506
engineers' misconceptions regarding
authentication, boundaries and

00:26:21.506 --> 00:26:25.616
inherent implementation complexity
make authentication issues prevalent.

00:26:26.306 --> 00:26:29.136
Methodologies of detecting
broken authentication are

00:26:29.136 --> 00:26:30.906
available and easy to create."

00:26:32.151 --> 00:26:36.621
The OWASP Impacts Description states
"Attackers can gain complete control

00:26:36.621 --> 00:26:41.451
of other users' accounts in the system,
read their personal data, and perform

00:26:41.451 --> 00:26:43.461
sensitive actions on their behalf.

00:26:43.971 --> 00:26:47.781
Systems are unlikely to be able
to distinguish attackers actions

00:26:47.786 --> 00:26:49.401
from legitimate user ones."

00:26:50.781 --> 00:26:54.561
Broken Authentication continues to be a
significant security issue due to poor

00:26:54.561 --> 00:26:58.791
password policies, weak authentication
mechanisms, and misconfigurations.

00:26:59.706 --> 00:27:03.036
API authentication is a complex
process that is commonly found with

00:27:03.036 --> 00:27:05.646
most APIs and is necessarily exposed.

00:27:06.246 --> 00:27:09.756
The impact of Broken Authentication can
lead to an attacker taking control of

00:27:09.786 --> 00:27:14.646
user accounts, compromising personal data,
and conducting sensitive actions like

00:27:14.646 --> 00:27:16.596
editing someone else's healthcare data.

00:27:17.256 --> 00:27:20.496
The authentication process is often
one of the first lines of defense

00:27:20.496 --> 00:27:24.276
for APIs, and when this mechanism is
vulnerable, it can lead to a data breach.

00:27:24.996 --> 00:27:27.786
A weak password policy does
not sufficiently protect user

00:27:27.786 --> 00:27:31.266
accounts by enforcing strong
password creation and management.

00:27:32.016 --> 00:27:36.156
This allows users to create simple
passwords, allows brute force attempts

00:27:36.156 --> 00:27:39.816
against user accounts, and allows
users to change their password without

00:27:39.816 --> 00:27:41.946
asking for password confirmation.

00:27:42.471 --> 00:27:46.951
Also allowing users to change their email
without asking for password confirmation.

00:27:47.581 --> 00:27:52.501
Weak password policies also disclose
tokens or passwords in the URL.

00:27:53.161 --> 00:27:56.221
It has a lack of authentication
for sensitive requests.

00:27:56.401 --> 00:28:00.481
And in regards to GraphQL queries,
weak password policies would

00:28:00.541 --> 00:28:03.451
allow for many authentication
attempts within a single request.

00:28:05.026 --> 00:28:07.996
Credential stuffing is a type of
attack against authentication where

00:28:07.996 --> 00:28:10.906
a large number of username and
password combinations are attempted.

00:28:11.476 --> 00:28:13.786
Credentials used in these
types of attacks are typically

00:28:13.786 --> 00:28:15.376
collected from data breaches.

00:28:16.456 --> 00:28:20.206
This sort of attack allows users to brute
force many different username and password

00:28:20.206 --> 00:28:21.736
combinations without restrictions.

00:28:23.266 --> 00:28:26.626
Predictable tokens refers to any
token obtained through a weak token

00:28:26.626 --> 00:28:28.426
generation authentication process.

00:28:28.786 --> 00:28:32.806
Weak tokens can easily be guessed,
deduced, or calculated by an attacker.

00:28:34.096 --> 00:28:39.816
This would be an issue if an API is using
incremental or guessable tokens for IDs.

00:28:41.391 --> 00:28:45.231
JWTs are commonly used
for API authentication and

00:28:45.231 --> 00:28:46.641
authorization processes.

00:28:46.911 --> 00:28:49.131
JWT stands for JSON Web Token.

00:28:49.851 --> 00:28:54.051
JWTs provide developers with the
flexibility to customize which algorithm

00:28:54.051 --> 00:28:58.851
is used for signing the token, the
key and secret that is used and the

00:28:58.851 --> 00:29:00.291
information used in the payload.

00:29:00.811 --> 00:29:03.691
This customization allows for
plenty of room for security

00:29:03.721 --> 00:29:05.251
misconfigurations to occur.

00:29:05.791 --> 00:29:11.851
These include API providers that accept
unsigned JWT tokens, API providers that do

00:29:11.851 --> 00:29:17.611
not check JWT expirations, API providers
that disclose sensitive information

00:29:17.611 --> 00:29:23.191
within the encoded JWT payload, and
if the JWT is signed with a weak key.

00:29:23.993 --> 00:29:27.893
API authentication can be a complex
system that includes several processes

00:29:27.893 --> 00:29:29.363
with a lot of room for failure.

00:29:29.813 --> 00:29:33.113
A couple decades ago, security
expert Bruce Schneier said, "the

00:29:33.113 --> 00:29:36.293
future of digital systems is
complexity, and complexity is

00:29:36.298 --> 00:29:37.703
the worst enemy of security."

00:29:38.243 --> 00:29:42.173
As we know from the six constraints
of REST APIs, RESTful APIs

00:29:42.173 --> 00:29:43.613
are designed to be stateless.

00:29:43.853 --> 00:29:47.153
To be stateless the API providers
shouldn't need to remember the

00:29:47.153 --> 00:29:48.983
consumer from one request to another.

00:29:49.558 --> 00:29:54.118
For this constraint to work, APIs often
require users to undergo a registration

00:29:54.118 --> 00:29:55.978
process to obtain a unique token.

00:29:56.668 --> 00:30:00.328
The token that is generated is then
used in subsequent requests for

00:30:00.328 --> 00:30:01.948
authentication and authorization.

00:30:03.158 --> 00:30:07.238
As a consequence, the registration
process used to obtain an API token,

00:30:07.538 --> 00:30:11.168
the token handling, and the system
that generates the token could all

00:30:11.168 --> 00:30:15.188
have their own set of weaknesses if
the token generation process doesn't

00:30:15.188 --> 00:30:19.493
rely on a high level of randomness,
or entropy, there's a chance that an

00:30:19.493 --> 00:30:23.693
attacker will be able to create their
own token or hijack another user's token.

00:30:24.563 --> 00:30:27.533
The other authentication processes
that could have their own set of

00:30:27.533 --> 00:30:31.733
vulnerabilities include aspects of the
registration system, like password reset

00:30:31.733 --> 00:30:33.593
and multifactor authentication features.

00:30:34.083 --> 00:30:37.773
For example, imagine a password
reset feature requiring you to

00:30:37.773 --> 00:30:41.253
provide an email address and a six
digit code to reset your password.

00:30:41.763 --> 00:30:44.793
While if the API allowed you to
make as many requests as you wanted,

00:30:44.913 --> 00:30:47.643
you'd only have to make 1 million
requests in order to guess the

00:30:47.643 --> 00:30:49.593
code and reset any user's password.

00:30:50.013 --> 00:30:53.163
A four digit code would
require only 10,000 requests.

00:30:54.513 --> 00:30:58.538
As it states in the OWASP Preventative
Measures, make sure you know all the

00:30:58.538 --> 00:31:01.208
possible flows to authenticate to the API.

00:31:01.958 --> 00:31:04.418
Ask your engineers what flows you missed.

00:31:04.958 --> 00:31:07.058
Read about your authentication mechanisms.

00:31:07.088 --> 00:31:10.178
Make sure you understand
what and how they are used.

00:31:10.478 --> 00:31:14.198
OAuth is not authentication
and neither are API keys.

00:31:14.438 --> 00:31:18.218
Don't reinvent the wheel in
authentication, token generation or

00:31:18.218 --> 00:31:20.558
password storage, use the standards.

00:31:21.473 --> 00:31:25.523
Credential recovery and forgot password
endpoints should be treated as login

00:31:25.523 --> 00:31:29.513
endpoints in terms of brute force,
rate-limiting and lockout protections.

00:31:29.903 --> 00:31:33.233
Require re-authentication for
sensitive operations, including

00:31:33.233 --> 00:31:36.413
changing the account on our email
address and multifactor phone number.

00:31:37.223 --> 00:31:39.533
Use the OWASP API
Authentication Cheat Sheet.

00:31:39.743 --> 00:31:41.483
You can find that in the written content.

00:31:42.263 --> 00:31:45.053
Where possible implement
multifactor authentication.

00:31:45.788 --> 00:31:49.388
Implement anti-brute force mechanisms
to mitigate credential stuffing,

00:31:49.448 --> 00:31:53.138
dictionary attacks, and brute force
attacks on your authentication endpoints.

00:31:53.618 --> 00:31:56.168
This mechanism should be
stricter than the regular rate

00:31:56.168 --> 00:31:57.938
limiting mechanisms on your APIs.

00:31:58.583 --> 00:32:02.213
Implement account lockout capture
mechanisms to prevent brute force

00:32:02.213 --> 00:32:04.193
attacks against specific users.

00:32:04.643 --> 00:32:06.853
Implement weak-password checks.

00:32:07.653 --> 00:32:09.863
API keys should not be
used for authentication.

00:32:10.013 --> 00:32:13.133
They should only be used for
API client authentication.

00:32:13.793 --> 00:32:16.673
Check out the written course
content for additional resources

00:32:16.673 --> 00:32:17.573
about Broken Authentication.

00:32:25.822 --> 00:32:31.082
API3:2023 Broken Object Property
Level Authorization, or BOPLA, is a

00:32:31.082 --> 00:32:36.422
combination of two items from the 2019
OWASP API Security Top 10: Excessive

00:32:36.422 --> 00:32:38.222
Data Exposure and Mass Assignment.

00:32:38.957 --> 00:32:42.527
Excessive Data Exposure takes place
when an API provider responds to a

00:32:42.527 --> 00:32:44.627
request with an entire data object.

00:32:45.047 --> 00:32:48.267
Usually an API provider will
filter out  the data object

00:32:48.267 --> 00:32:49.827
down to what is being requested.

00:32:50.367 --> 00:32:53.787
When the data object is shared without
being filtered, there is an increased

00:32:53.787 --> 00:32:55.887
risk of exposing sensitive information.

00:32:56.647 --> 00:32:59.767
Mass Assignment is a weakness
that allows for user input to

00:32:59.767 --> 00:33:01.897
alter sensitive object properties.

00:33:02.137 --> 00:33:06.787
If, for example, an API uses a special
property to create admin accounts,

00:33:07.117 --> 00:33:10.627
only authorized users should be able
to make requests that successfully

00:33:10.627 --> 00:33:12.697
alter those administrative properties.

00:33:13.027 --> 00:33:16.357
If there are no restrictions in
place, then an attacker would be

00:33:16.357 --> 00:33:19.717
able to elevate their privileges
and perform administrative actions

00:33:19.722 --> 00:33:21.067
by altering those properties.

00:33:21.807 --> 00:33:25.557
Both of these vulnerabilities
involved issues with object property

00:33:25.557 --> 00:33:29.427
authorization, so they're combined
under the new title of Broken

00:33:29.427 --> 00:33:31.287
Object Property Level Authorization.

00:33:32.547 --> 00:33:37.844
As it states in the OWASP Attack Vector
description: APIs tend to expose endpoints

00:33:37.844 --> 00:33:39.974
that return all objects properties.

00:33:40.334 --> 00:33:43.094
This is particularly valid for REST APIs.

00:33:43.244 --> 00:33:48.004
For other protocols such as GraphQL, it
may require  crafted requests to specify

00:33:48.004 --> 00:33:49.654
which properties should be returned.

00:33:50.494 --> 00:33:54.274
Identifying these additional properties
that can be manipulated requires more

00:33:54.274 --> 00:33:58.474
effort, but there are a few automated
tools available to assist in this task.

00:33:59.014 --> 00:34:03.754
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states in inspecting API responses is

00:34:03.754 --> 00:34:08.524
enough to identify sensitive information
in returned objects, representations.

00:34:08.879 --> 00:34:12.899
Fuzzing is usually used to identify
additional hidden properties.

00:34:13.169 --> 00:34:16.739
Whether they can be changed is a
matter of crafting an API request

00:34:16.739 --> 00:34:18.239
and analyzing the response.

00:34:18.479 --> 00:34:21.689
Side effect analysis may be
required if the target property is

00:34:21.694 --> 00:34:23.549
not returned in the API response.

00:34:24.334 --> 00:34:28.654
The OWASP Impacts Description states
unauthorized access to private

00:34:28.684 --> 00:34:32.494
or sensitive object properties
may result in data disclosure,

00:34:32.704 --> 00:34:34.714
data loss, or data corruption.

00:34:35.164 --> 00:34:39.064
Under certain circumstances, unauthorized
access to object properties can

00:34:39.069 --> 00:34:43.534
lead to privilege as escalation or
partial to full account takeover.

00:34:44.770 --> 00:34:48.040
As previously stated, Broken Object
Property Level Authorization, or

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:51.410
BOPLA, is a combination of Mass
Assignment  and Excessive Data Exposure.

00:34:51.770 --> 00:34:54.920
In the 2023 release notes, the
security project states that these two

00:34:54.920 --> 00:34:59.270
vulnerabilities were combined focusing on
the common root cause, object, property

00:34:59.270 --> 00:35:01.370
level authorization, validation failures.

00:35:01.850 --> 00:35:07.400
The OWASP API Security project also states
an API endpoint is vulnerable if either

00:35:07.550 --> 00:35:11.900
the API endpoint exposes properties of an
object that are considered sensitive and

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:16.820
should not be read by the user, which is
an Excessive Data Exposure, or the API

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:21.020
endpoint allows a user to change, add, or
delete the value of a sensitive object's

00:35:21.025 --> 00:35:24.980
property, which the user should not be
able to access, which is Mass Assignment.

00:35:25.670 --> 00:35:29.900
Excessive Data Exposure is when an API
endpoint responds with more information

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:31.730
than is needed to fulfill a request.

00:35:31.970 --> 00:35:35.150
This often occurs in cases when
the provider expects the API

00:35:35.270 --> 00:35:37.100
consumer to filter results.

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:41.240
When a consumer requests specific
information, the provider might respond

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:45.410
with all sorts of information, assuming
the consumer will then remove any data

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:47.120
they don't need from the response.

00:35:47.450 --> 00:35:51.230
When this vulnerability is present, it can
be the equivalent of asking someone for

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:54.710
their name and having them respond with
their name, date of birth, email address,

00:35:54.710 --> 00:35:57.890
phone number, and the identification
of every other person they know.

00:35:58.665 --> 00:36:02.565
For example, if an API consumer
requests information for their user

00:36:02.565 --> 00:36:05.955
account and receives information
about other user accounts as well,

00:36:06.075 --> 00:36:09.255
the API is exposing excessive data.

00:36:09.765 --> 00:36:12.525
Check out the written content
for an example of an excessive

00:36:12.525 --> 00:36:13.965
data exposure vulnerability.

00:36:15.095 --> 00:36:18.755
Excessive Data Exposure is an API
vulnerability that is very difficult

00:36:18.755 --> 00:36:20.435
to detect with automated scanners.

00:36:20.705 --> 00:36:24.485
In addition, this vulnerability will
often bypass every security control in

00:36:24.485 --> 00:36:29.075
place to protect sensitive information
and hand sensitive data to an attacker

00:36:29.080 --> 00:36:30.595
simply because they use the API.

00:36:31.475 --> 00:36:34.945
To detect excessive data
exposure API providers must test

00:36:34.945 --> 00:36:38.345
API endpoints and review the
information sent in the response.

00:36:38.764 --> 00:36:42.874
Mass Assignment occurs when an API
consumer includes more parameters in its

00:36:42.874 --> 00:36:46.984
request than the application intended,
and the application adds these parameters

00:36:46.984 --> 00:36:49.174
to code variables or internal objects.

00:36:49.504 --> 00:36:52.744
In this situation, a consumer may
be able to edit object properties

00:36:52.804 --> 00:36:54.064
or escalate the privileges.

00:36:54.634 --> 00:36:58.414
For example, an application might have
an account update functionality that

00:36:58.414 --> 00:37:02.644
the user should use only to update
their username, password, and address.

00:37:02.914 --> 00:37:07.174
If the consumer can include additional
parameters in the request related to their

00:37:07.174 --> 00:37:11.344
account, such as the account privilege
level, or sensitive information like

00:37:11.374 --> 00:37:15.364
an account balance, and the application
accepts those parameters without checking

00:37:15.364 --> 00:37:19.534
them against the whitelist of permitted
actions, the consumer could take advantage

00:37:19.534 --> 00:37:21.484
of this weakness to change these values.

00:37:22.114 --> 00:37:24.904
Imagine an API is called
to create an account with

00:37:24.904 --> 00:37:26.854
parameters for user and password.

00:37:27.364 --> 00:37:30.944
The user could be hapi_hacker,
password could be GreatPassword123.

00:37:31.414 --> 00:37:35.854
While reading the API documentation
regarding the account creation process,

00:37:35.854 --> 00:37:39.304
an attacker could discover that there's
an additional property, which is

00:37:39.304 --> 00:37:43.684
an admin that the API provider uses
to create administrative accounts.

00:37:43.984 --> 00:37:47.764
An attacker could add this to a
request and set the value to "true".

00:37:48.214 --> 00:37:53.224
If the API does not sanitize the request
input, it is vulnerable to Mass Assignment

00:37:53.224 --> 00:37:56.944
and attacker could use the request
to create their own admin account.

00:37:57.334 --> 00:38:01.444
On the backend the vulnerable web
app will add the key value attribute

00:38:01.444 --> 00:38:06.334
{“isAdmin”: true} to the user object,
and make the user the equivalent of

00:38:06.334 --> 00:38:07.984
an administrator for that application.

00:38:08.585 --> 00:38:12.335
The OWASP Preventative Measures
state when exposing an object using

00:38:12.335 --> 00:38:16.385
an API endpoint, always make sure
that the user should have access to

00:38:16.390 --> 00:38:18.065
the object properties you expose.

00:38:18.635 --> 00:38:22.525
Avoid using generic methods such
as to_json() and to_string().

00:38:22.805 --> 00:38:25.655
Instead, cherry-pick specific
object properties you

00:38:25.655 --> 00:38:27.125
specifically want to return.

00:38:27.575 --> 00:38:31.595
If possible, avoid using functions
that automatically bind to client's

00:38:31.595 --> 00:38:35.795
input into code variables, internal
objects or object properties.

00:38:36.155 --> 00:38:39.215
Allow changes only to the
object's properties that should

00:38:39.215 --> 00:38:40.595
be updated by the client.

00:38:41.365 --> 00:38:44.769
Implement a schema based
response validation mechanism

00:38:44.769 --> 00:38:46.359
as an extra layer of security.

00:38:46.959 --> 00:38:51.819
As part of this mechanism, define and
enforce data returned by all API methods.

00:38:52.389 --> 00:38:56.259
Keep return data structures to the
bare minimum according to the business

00:38:56.259 --> 00:38:58.629
functional requirements for the endpoint.

00:38:59.709 --> 00:39:03.179
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about BOPLA.

00:39:10.835 --> 00:39:16.065
API4:2023 Unrestricted Resource
Consumption is an API issue where

00:39:16.065 --> 00:39:18.945
the provider of the API does
not have safeguards in place to

00:39:18.945 --> 00:39:21.185
prevent excessive use of their API.

00:39:21.735 --> 00:39:25.485
When there are no restrictions for
resource consumption, the API provider

00:39:25.485 --> 00:39:28.965
could become a victim of a denial
of service, attack, or experience,

00:39:28.965 --> 00:39:30.795
unnecessary financial costs.

00:39:31.125 --> 00:39:35.985
Unrestricted Resource Consumption
is an updated version of API4:2019,

00:39:36.315 --> 00:39:37.995
Lack of Resources and Rate Limiting.

00:39:38.980 --> 00:39:45.050
The OWASP Attack Vector description states
exploitation requires simple API requests.

00:39:45.260 --> 00:39:49.610
Multiple concurrent requests can be
performed from a single local computer

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:52.010
or by cloud computing resources.

00:39:52.400 --> 00:39:55.220
Most of the automated tools
available are designed to cause

00:39:55.220 --> 00:39:59.270
denial of service via high loads of
traffic impacting API service rate.

00:40:00.095 --> 00:40:03.545
The OWASP Security Weakness
description states it's common to

00:40:03.545 --> 00:40:07.955
find APIs that do not limit client
interactions or resource consumption.

00:40:08.345 --> 00:40:11.915
Crafted API requests such as those,
including parameters that control

00:40:11.915 --> 00:40:15.095
the number of resources to be
returned and performing response

00:40:15.095 --> 00:40:19.685
status, time length analysis, should
allow identification of the issue.

00:40:20.195 --> 00:40:23.915
The same is valid for batched
operations, although threat agents

00:40:23.915 --> 00:40:27.875
don't have visibility over costs
impact, this can be inferred based

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:29.357
on the service provider's business.

00:40:30.146 --> 00:40:34.614
OWASP Impacts Description states
exploitation can lead to denial of

00:40:34.614 --> 00:40:39.024
service due to resource starvation, but
it can also lead to operational cost

00:40:39.024 --> 00:40:44.494
increase, such as those related due to the
infrastructure due to higher CPU demand,

00:40:44.499 --> 00:40:46.614
increasing cloud storage needs, etc.

00:40:47.254 --> 00:40:51.034
The OWASP API Security project
states an API is vulnerable if at

00:40:51.034 --> 00:40:54.334
least one of the following limits
is missing or set inappropriately,

00:40:54.364 --> 00:40:55.984
either too low or too high.

00:40:56.614 --> 00:41:00.994
Execution timeouts, maximum allocable
memory, maximum number of file

00:41:00.999 --> 00:41:05.719
descriptors, maximum number of
processes, maximum upload file size

00:41:05.959 --> 00:41:10.069
number of operations to perform in
a single API client request with

00:41:10.069 --> 00:41:14.629
things like GraphQL batching, number
of records per page to return in a

00:41:14.629 --> 00:41:18.559
single request response, and third
party service provider spending limit.

00:41:19.624 --> 00:41:24.754
Every API request has a technical and
financial cost when API providers do

00:41:24.754 --> 00:41:28.204
not enforce limitations on resource
consumption, there's an increased

00:41:28.204 --> 00:41:32.074
risk of denial of service, distributed
denial of service, unnecessary

00:41:32.074 --> 00:41:36.034
financial costs, and degradation of
the quality of service to other users.

00:41:36.504 --> 00:41:39.924
In addition rate limiting plays an
important role in the monetization

00:41:39.974 --> 00:41:41.924
and availability of APIs.

00:41:42.254 --> 00:41:46.664
Many API providers monetize their APIs
by limiting requests and allowing paid

00:41:46.664 --> 00:41:49.244
customers to request more information.

00:41:50.154 --> 00:41:54.414
RapidAPI, for example, allows
some API providers to also have

00:41:54.414 --> 00:41:58.134
infrastructure that automatically
scales with the number of API requests.

00:41:58.434 --> 00:42:01.764
In these cases, an unlimited
number of requests would lead to a

00:42:01.764 --> 00:42:05.964
significant and easily preventable
increase in infrastructure costs.

00:42:06.457 --> 00:42:10.327
The OWASP Preventative Measures
states Docker makes it easy to limit

00:42:10.327 --> 00:42:14.437
memory, CPU, number of restarts,
file descriptors and processes.

00:42:14.707 --> 00:42:17.587
Implement a limit on how often
a client can call the API

00:42:17.767 --> 00:42:19.087
within a defined timeframe.

00:42:19.537 --> 00:42:23.677
Notify the client when the limit is
exceeded by providing the limit number and

00:42:23.677 --> 00:42:25.947
the time at which the limit will be reset.

00:42:26.562 --> 00:42:29.862
Add proper server side validation
for query string and request body

00:42:29.862 --> 00:42:33.402
parameters, specifically the one
that controls the number of records

00:42:33.402 --> 00:42:34.992
to be returned in the response.

00:42:35.292 --> 00:42:40.722
Define and enforce maximum size of data
on all incoming parameters and payloads,

00:42:40.727 --> 00:42:45.102
such as maximum length for strings and
maximum number of elements in arrays.

00:42:45.582 --> 00:42:48.732
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about

00:42:48.732 --> 00:42:50.412
Unrestricted Resource Consumption.

00:42:56.882 --> 00:43:01.772
API5:2023, Broken Function Level
Authorization, or BFLA, is a

00:43:01.772 --> 00:43:05.432
vulnerability where API functions
have insufficient access controls.

00:43:06.172 --> 00:43:11.402
Where BOLA is about access to data, BFLA
is about altering or deleting that data.

00:43:11.662 --> 00:43:16.002
In addition, a vulnerable API would allow
an attacker to perform actions of other

00:43:16.002 --> 00:43:18.012
roles, including administrative actions.

00:43:18.462 --> 00:43:23.212
To drive the point home, a fintech
API susceptible to BOLA would allow an

00:43:23.212 --> 00:43:27.642
attacker the ability to see what is in
the bank account of another user, while

00:43:27.642 --> 00:43:31.852
the same API vulnerable to BFLA would
allow an attacker to transfer funds

00:43:31.852 --> 00:43:33.612
from other users accounts to their own.

00:43:34.502 --> 00:43:38.682
The OWASP Attack Vector description
states "exploitation requires the

00:43:38.682 --> 00:43:42.672
attacker to send legitimate API calls
to an API endpoint that they should

00:43:42.672 --> 00:43:47.732
not have access to as an anonymous
user or regular non privileged user.

00:43:48.432 --> 00:43:50.832
exposed endpoints will
be easily exploited."

00:43:51.722 --> 00:43:55.582
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states "authorization checks for

00:43:55.582 --> 00:43:59.772
a function or resource are usually
managed via configuration or code level.

00:44:00.172 --> 00:44:04.132
Implementing proper checks can be a
confusing task since modern applications

00:44:04.132 --> 00:44:08.272
can contain many types of roles,
groups, and complex user hierarchies.

00:44:09.392 --> 00:44:13.182
It's easier to discover these
flaws in APIs since APIs are more

00:44:13.182 --> 00:44:16.872
structured, and accessing different
functions is more predictable."

00:44:17.572 --> 00:44:21.732
The OWASP Impacts Description states
"such flaws allow attackers to

00:44:21.732 --> 00:44:23.682
access unauthorized functionality.

00:44:24.122 --> 00:44:27.332
Administrative functions are key
targets for this type of attack

00:44:27.352 --> 00:44:31.692
and may lead to data disclosure,
data loss, or data corruption.

00:44:32.022 --> 00:44:34.612
Ultimately, it may lead
to service disruption."

00:44:35.565 --> 00:44:39.045
Broken Function Level Authorization,
or BFLA, is a vulnerability where a

00:44:39.045 --> 00:44:43.835
user of one privilege level can use the
API functionality of a different user,

00:44:43.965 --> 00:44:46.205
user group, or another privilege level.

00:44:46.845 --> 00:44:50.085
API providers will often have
different privilege levels for

00:44:50.085 --> 00:44:53.675
different types of accounts, such as
public users, merchants, partners,

00:44:53.685 --> 00:44:56.055
vendors, administrators, and so on.

00:44:56.825 --> 00:45:01.555
BFLA can be exploited for unauthorized
use of lateral functions or a similarly

00:45:01.555 --> 00:45:05.265
privileged group, or it could be
exploited for privilege escalation,

00:45:05.475 --> 00:45:08.685
where an attacker can use the
functions of a more privileged group.

00:45:09.445 --> 00:45:13.615
Particularly interesting API functions
to access include those that deal

00:45:13.615 --> 00:45:17.055
with sensitive information, resources
that belong to another group,

00:45:17.480 --> 00:45:20.620
and administrative functionality,
like user account management.

00:45:21.610 --> 00:45:25.300
If an API has different privilege
levels, it may use different endpoints

00:45:25.330 --> 00:45:27.030
to perform those privileged actions.

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:33.030
For example, a bank may use
/{userid}/account/balance as an

00:45:33.050 --> 00:45:37.140
endpoint for a user wishing to access
their account information, and then

00:45:37.140 --> 00:45:43.790
use /admin/account/{userid} as an
endpoint for an administrator that needs

00:45:43.790 --> 00:45:45.700
to access user account information.

00:45:46.300 --> 00:45:49.290
If the application does not
have access controls implemented

00:45:49.290 --> 00:45:52.820
correctly, an attacker will be able
to perform administrative actions

00:45:52.820 --> 00:45:54.440
and perform an account takeover.

00:45:54.930 --> 00:45:57.960
An API won't always use
administrative endpoints for

00:45:57.960 --> 00:45:59.230
administrative functionality.

00:45:59.580 --> 00:46:03.580
Instead, the functionality could be
based on different HTTP request methods

00:46:03.730 --> 00:46:06.060
like GET, POST, PUT, and DELETE.

00:46:06.700 --> 00:46:10.860
If a provider doesn't restrict the
HTTP methods, or verbs, an attacker

00:46:10.860 --> 00:46:15.900
can use simply making an unauthorized
request with a different method,

00:46:16.120 --> 00:46:18.190
could indicate a BFLA vulnerability.

00:46:18.870 --> 00:46:22.080
When testing for BFLA, look for
any functionality that an attacker

00:46:22.080 --> 00:46:23.380
could use to their advantage.

00:46:23.760 --> 00:46:27.410
These functions include, but are not
limited to, altering user accounts,

00:46:27.860 --> 00:46:31.550
deleting user resources, and gaining
access to restricted endpoints.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:36.940
For example, if an API gave partners
the ability to add new users to the

00:46:36.940 --> 00:46:42.420
partner group, but did not restrict this
functionality to the specific group, any

00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:44.600
user could add themselves to any group.

00:46:45.310 --> 00:46:48.580
Moreover, if an attacker added
themselves to a group, there's a good

00:46:48.580 --> 00:46:51.980
chance that there'll be able to access
that group's sensitive resources.

00:46:52.540 --> 00:46:56.110
Take a look at the written course
content for an example of a BFLA attack.

00:47:02.986 --> 00:47:06.566
The OWASP Preventative Measures
state "your application should have

00:47:06.566 --> 00:47:10.746
a consistent and easy to analyze
authorization module that is invoked

00:47:10.756 --> 00:47:12.226
from all your business functions.

00:47:12.886 --> 00:47:16.636
Frequently, such protection is
provided by one or more components

00:47:16.706 --> 00:47:18.376
external to the application code.

00:47:18.961 --> 00:47:23.741
The enforcement mechanisms should
deny all access by default, requiring

00:47:23.961 --> 00:47:28.181
explicit grants to specific roles
for access to every function.

00:47:28.621 --> 00:47:33.051
Review your API endpoints against
function level authorization flaws, while

00:47:33.051 --> 00:47:37.041
keeping in mind the business logic of
the application and group's hierarchy.

00:47:37.581 --> 00:47:40.751
Make sure that all of your
administrative controllers inherit from

00:47:40.751 --> 00:47:44.971
an administrative abstract controller
that implements authorization checks

00:47:45.116 --> 00:47:47.326
based on the user's group or role.

00:47:47.956 --> 00:47:51.306
Make sure that administrative
functions inside a regular controller

00:47:51.326 --> 00:47:55.026
implement authorization checks
based on the user's group and role."

00:47:55.746 --> 00:47:58.756
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about

00:47:58.786 --> 00:48:00.596
Broken Function Level Authorization.

00:48:05.726 --> 00:48:10.986
API6:2023, Unrestricted Access to
Sensitive Business Flows represents

00:48:10.986 --> 00:48:15.466
the risk of an attacker being able to
identify and exploit API driven workflows.

00:48:16.076 --> 00:48:19.896
If vulnerable an attacker will be able
to leverage an organization's API request

00:48:19.896 --> 00:48:21.986
structure to obstruct other users.

00:48:22.456 --> 00:48:25.986
This obstruction could come in the form
of spamming other users, depleting the

00:48:25.986 --> 00:48:29.706
stock of highly sought after items,
or preventing other users from using

00:48:29.706 --> 00:48:31.886
expected application functionality.

00:48:32.536 --> 00:48:35.166
This is a new addition
to the 2023 top 10 list.

00:48:35.866 --> 00:48:40.446
The OWASP Attack Vector description
states "exploitation usually involves

00:48:40.466 --> 00:48:45.116
understanding the business model backed
by the API, finding sensitive business

00:48:45.116 --> 00:48:49.086
flows, and automating access to these
flows, causing harm to the business."

00:48:50.251 --> 00:48:54.651
The Security Weakness description
states "lack of a holistic view of the

00:48:54.651 --> 00:48:58.511
API in order to fully support business
requirements tends to contribute

00:48:58.511 --> 00:48:59.801
to the prevalence of this issue.

00:49:00.531 --> 00:49:05.011
Attackers manually identify what resources
or endpoints are involved in the target

00:49:05.011 --> 00:49:06.991
workflow and how they work together.

00:49:07.491 --> 00:49:11.631
If mitigation mechanisms are
already in place, attackers need

00:49:11.631 --> 00:49:13.141
to find a way to bypass them."

00:49:13.751 --> 00:49:19.081
The OWASP Impacts Description states "in
general, technical impact is not expected.

00:49:19.401 --> 00:49:22.061
Exploitation might hurt the
business in different ways.

00:49:22.351 --> 00:49:26.421
For example, prevalent legitimate
users from purchasing a product

00:49:26.831 --> 00:49:30.961
or lead to an inflation in the
internal economy of a game.

00:49:31.673 --> 00:49:35.423
Regarding APIs, a flow is a
series of requests and responses

00:49:35.423 --> 00:49:36.833
that lead to an operation.

00:49:37.233 --> 00:49:41.173
If, for example, a purchase flow for a
web application does not restrict access

00:49:41.173 --> 00:49:44.953
to a purchase process, then a scalper
could automate requests to instantly drain

00:49:44.953 --> 00:49:46.543
the stock of an item down to nothing.

00:49:47.093 --> 00:49:51.293
This is where mechanisms like a completely
automated public Turing test to tell

00:49:51.293 --> 00:49:56.203
computers and humans apart, or better
known as CAPTCHA, comes into play.

00:49:56.963 --> 00:50:01.213
If a flow has a capture mechanism that
requires human interaction, then the

00:50:01.233 --> 00:50:05.593
automated request could be interrupted
and slow down automated purchasing.

00:50:06.193 --> 00:50:09.933
Check out the written course material
for an example API flow used to help

00:50:09.983 --> 00:50:12.293
build a notification bot for the PS5.

00:50:12.998 --> 00:50:17.048
Customers and or scalpers competing
to purchase the PS5 would use API

00:50:17.048 --> 00:50:20.638
flows to either complete purchases
as soon as new stock was available

00:50:20.838 --> 00:50:22.938
or alert upon stock updates.

00:50:23.578 --> 00:50:27.238
In the example, an API flow was used
to automatically check for stock

00:50:27.238 --> 00:50:29.028
updates and send out email alerts.

00:50:30.034 --> 00:50:33.844
The OWASP Preventative Measures state
the mitigation planning should be done

00:50:33.844 --> 00:50:36.924
in two layers, business and engineering.

00:50:37.434 --> 00:50:40.784
Business should identify the business
flows that might harm the business

00:50:40.784 --> 00:50:42.164
if they are excessively used.

00:50:42.474 --> 00:50:45.144
Engineering should choose the
right protection mechanism to

00:50:45.144 --> 00:50:46.504
mitigate the business risk.

00:50:47.054 --> 00:50:50.294
Some of the protection mechanisms
are more simple while others

00:50:50.304 --> 00:50:51.774
are more difficult to implement.

00:50:52.304 --> 00:50:55.204
The following methods are used
to slow down automated threats.

00:50:55.784 --> 00:50:57.054
Device fingerprinting.

00:50:57.354 --> 00:51:01.819
Deny service to unexpected client
devices, tends to make threat

00:51:01.829 --> 00:51:06.009
actors use more sophisticated
solutions, thus more costly for them.

00:51:06.779 --> 00:51:10.719
Next up is human detection
using either CAPTCHA or more

00:51:10.729 --> 00:51:12.399
advanced biometric solutions.

00:51:13.799 --> 00:51:15.039
Non human patterns.

00:51:15.089 --> 00:51:18.629
Analyze the user flow to
detect non human patterns.

00:51:19.739 --> 00:51:23.399
For example, the user accessed the
add to cart and complete purchase

00:51:23.409 --> 00:51:25.149
functions in less than one second.

00:51:25.859 --> 00:51:30.479
Consider blocking IP addresses of Tor
exit nodes and well known proxies.

00:51:30.989 --> 00:51:34.729
Secure and limit access to APIs that
are consumed directly by machines.

00:51:35.429 --> 00:51:38.149
They tend to be an easy target
for attackers because they

00:51:38.159 --> 00:51:41.249
often don't implement all the
required protection mechanisms.

00:51:41.809 --> 00:51:46.079
Check out the written course content for
additional resources about unrestricted

00:51:46.089 --> 00:51:48.059
access to sensitive business flows.

00:51:51.117 --> 00:51:56.527
API7:2023, Server Side Request Forgery
is a vulnerability that takes place when

00:51:56.527 --> 00:52:00.587
a user is able to control the remote
resources retrieved by an application.

00:52:01.077 --> 00:52:05.787
An attacker can use an API to supply
their own input in the form of a URL

00:52:05.827 --> 00:52:09.327
to control the remote resources that
are retrieved by the targeted server.

00:52:09.927 --> 00:52:14.197
An attacker could supply URLs that
expose private data, scan the target's

00:52:14.207 --> 00:52:18.402
internal network, or compromise the
target through remote code execution.

00:52:18.832 --> 00:52:23.692
SSRF is also number 10 on the
2021 OWASP Top 10 List and

00:52:23.692 --> 00:52:25.492
is a growing threat to APIs.

00:52:26.722 --> 00:52:31.462
The OWASP Attack Vector description
states "exploitation requires the attacker

00:52:31.462 --> 00:52:37.332
to find an API endpoint that accesses
a URI that's provided by the client.

00:52:37.722 --> 00:52:43.512
In general, basic SSRF, when the response
is returned by the attacker, is easier

00:52:43.512 --> 00:52:48.522
to exploit than blind SSRF, in which
the attacker has no feedback on whether

00:52:48.522 --> 00:52:50.132
or not the attack was successful."

00:52:51.172 --> 00:52:55.947
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states "modern concepts in application

00:52:55.947 --> 00:53:00.387
development encourages developers to
access URIs provided by the client.

00:53:00.987 --> 00:53:05.517
Lack of, or improper validation
of such URIs are common issues.

00:53:05.997 --> 00:53:10.707
Regular API requests and response analysis
will be required to detect the issue.

00:53:11.287 --> 00:53:14.807
When the response is not returned,
detecting the vulnerability requires

00:53:14.817 --> 00:53:15.937
more effort and creativity."

00:53:17.357 --> 00:53:21.747
The OWASP Impacts Description states
"successful exploitation might lead

00:53:21.747 --> 00:53:26.507
to internal services enumeration,
information disclosure, bypassing

00:53:26.517 --> 00:53:29.037
firewalls, or other security mechanisms.

00:53:29.477 --> 00:53:33.267
In some cases, it can lead to denial
of service or the server being used as

00:53:33.267 --> 00:53:35.387
a proxy to hide malicious activities."

00:53:36.320 --> 00:53:40.030
SSRF is a vulnerability that takes place
when an application retrieves remote

00:53:40.030 --> 00:53:42.360
resources without validating user input.

00:53:42.870 --> 00:53:46.920
When an attacker has control over the
resources a server requests, then they can

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:51.550
gain access to sensitive data, or worse,
completely compromise a vulnerable host.

00:53:52.070 --> 00:53:55.020
The impact of this vulnerability is
that an attacker would be able to

00:53:55.020 --> 00:53:59.270
leverage a target server to perform
and process requests that they supply.

00:53:59.880 --> 00:54:04.350
Note that  bug bounty payouts for SSRF
are driven based on the impact that can

00:54:04.350 --> 00:54:06.780
be demonstrated with a proof of concept.

00:54:07.140 --> 00:54:10.090
The higher the demonstrated
impact, the higher the bounty.

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:13.760
There are two general types
of SSRF that are worth noting.

00:54:14.880 --> 00:54:18.680
In-Band SSRF and Out-of-Band
SSRF, also known as Blind SSRF.

00:54:18.680 --> 00:54:23.930
In-Band SSRF means that the
server responds with the resources

00:54:23.950 --> 00:54:26.040
specified by the end user.

00:54:26.370 --> 00:54:30.850
If the attacker specifies a payload
such as google.com to a server with an

00:54:30.850 --> 00:54:35.660
In-Band SSRF vulnerability, the server
would make the request to google.com

00:54:35.680 --> 00:54:40.140
and then respond to the attacker with
information served from google.com.

00:54:41.050 --> 00:54:45.060
Blind SSRF takes place when the
attacker supplies a URL and the server

00:54:45.060 --> 00:54:50.130
makes the request, but the server does
not send the information back from

00:54:50.130 --> 00:54:52.870
that specified URL to the attacker.

00:54:53.360 --> 00:54:58.080
In the case of blind SSRF, an attacker
would need control over a web server

00:54:58.410 --> 00:55:02.150
that would capture the request from the
target as proof that they were able to

00:55:02.150 --> 00:55:04.120
force the server to make this request.

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:08.450
In the course In-Band SSRF
example, an attacker makes a

00:55:08.450 --> 00:55:10.030
request that includes a URL.

00:55:10.350 --> 00:55:13.660
That will be used to update the
stock of an item in the store.

00:55:14.020 --> 00:55:18.590
The attacker is able to replace that
item stock URL with a local host

00:55:18.590 --> 00:55:20.620
URL to a directory with a password.

00:55:20.980 --> 00:55:24.830
The server is vulnerable and provides
the password back to the attacker.

00:55:25.795 --> 00:55:30.315
Blind SSRF takes place when a vulnerable
server performs a request from user input,

00:55:30.315 --> 00:55:34.155
but does not send back the response to the
attacker indicating a successful attack.

00:55:34.665 --> 00:55:37.975
In other words, the victim server
makes the request to the URL

00:55:37.985 --> 00:55:42.015
specified by the attacker, but the
attacker does not receive a direct

00:55:42.015 --> 00:55:43.805
message back from the victim server.

00:55:44.135 --> 00:55:48.155
In this case, to know if the request
was made, an attacker will need to

00:55:48.155 --> 00:55:51.645
have some control over the web server
that is specified in the attack.

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:57.320
For examples and demonstrations of
In-Band and Out-of-Band SSRF attacks

00:55:57.470 --> 00:55:58.990
check out the written course material.

00:56:00.181 --> 00:56:03.561
The OWASP Preventative Measure
states "isolate the resource

00:56:03.561 --> 00:56:05.161
fetching mechanism in your network.

00:56:05.331 --> 00:56:07.731
Usually these features are
aimed to retrieve remote

00:56:07.741 --> 00:56:09.311
resources and not internal ones.

00:56:09.801 --> 00:56:15.231
Whenever possible, use allow lists
of remote origins users are expected

00:56:15.231 --> 00:56:19.491
to download resources from, such
as Google Drive or Gravatar, URL

00:56:19.491 --> 00:56:23.941
schemes and ports, accepted media
types for a given functionality.

00:56:24.921 --> 00:56:26.971
Disable HTTP redirections.

00:56:27.401 --> 00:56:31.381
Use a well tested and maintained
URL parser to avoid issues caused

00:56:31.381 --> 00:56:33.631
by URL parsing inconsistencies.

00:56:34.171 --> 00:56:37.431
Validate and sanitize all
client supplied input data.

00:56:38.061 --> 00:56:40.071
Do not send raw responses to clients."

00:56:40.961 --> 00:56:44.421
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about SSRF.

00:56:51.901 --> 00:56:56.011
API8:2023, Security Misconfiguration
represents a catch-all for

00:56:56.011 --> 00:56:59.151
many vulnerabilities related
to the systems that host APIs.

00:56:59.671 --> 00:57:02.991
When an API's security is misconfigured,
it can be detrimental to the

00:57:02.991 --> 00:57:07.001
confidentiality, integrity, and
availability of the API provider's data.

00:57:07.681 --> 00:57:11.521
Due to the wide variety of flaws that
could exist, the impacts of an exploited

00:57:11.521 --> 00:57:16.371
security misconfiguration can range from
information disclosure to data breach.

00:57:16.946 --> 00:57:20.896
The OWASP Attack Vector description
states "attackers will often attempt

00:57:20.906 --> 00:57:24.706
to find unpatched flaws, common
endpoints, or unprotected files and

00:57:24.706 --> 00:57:28.106
directories to gain unauthorized
access or knowledge of the system."

00:57:28.926 --> 00:57:32.746
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states "security misconfiguration

00:57:32.746 --> 00:57:35.836
can happen at any level of
the API stack from the network

00:57:35.836 --> 00:57:37.566
level to the application level.

00:57:37.996 --> 00:57:42.596
Automated tools are available to detect
and exploit misconfiguration such as

00:57:42.616 --> 00:57:44.786
unnecessary services or legacy options."

00:57:45.606 --> 00:57:50.046
The OWASP Impacts Description states
"security misconfigurations can not

00:57:50.066 --> 00:57:54.546
only expose sensitive user data,
but also system details that can

00:57:54.546 --> 00:57:56.316
lead to full server compromise."

00:57:56.687 --> 00:58:00.707
Security misconfigurations include all
the mistakes that API providers can make

00:58:00.707 --> 00:58:02.767
within the supporting systems of an API.

00:58:03.197 --> 00:58:06.647
Security misconfigurations are really
a set of weaknesses that include

00:58:06.897 --> 00:58:10.767
misconfigured headers, misconfigured
transit encryption, the use of

00:58:10.767 --> 00:58:15.837
default accounts, the acceptance of
unnecessary HTTP methods, a lack of input

00:58:15.887 --> 00:58:18.627
sanitization, and verbose error messaging.

00:58:19.017 --> 00:58:22.607
For example, if the API supporting
security configuration reveals

00:58:22.607 --> 00:58:24.087
an unpatched vulnerability.

00:58:24.487 --> 00:58:27.607
There's a chance that an attacker
could leverage a published exploit to

00:58:27.607 --> 00:58:29.607
easily pwn the API and its systems.

00:58:30.397 --> 00:58:33.857
A lack of input sanitization
could allow attackers to upload

00:58:33.897 --> 00:58:35.497
malicious payloads to the server.

00:58:35.947 --> 00:58:40.197
APIs often play a key role in automating
processes, so imagine being able

00:58:40.197 --> 00:58:44.597
to upload payloads that the server
automatically processes into a format

00:58:44.617 --> 00:58:48.787
that could be remotely executed or
executed by an unsuspecting end user.

00:58:49.602 --> 00:58:53.562
For example, if an upload endpoint
was used to pass uploaded files

00:58:53.562 --> 00:58:57.062
to a web directory, then it could
allow the upload of a script.

00:58:57.462 --> 00:59:01.572
Navigating to the URL where the file is
located could launch the script resulting

00:59:01.572 --> 00:59:03.462
in direct shell access to the web server.

00:59:04.022 --> 00:59:08.037
Additionally, a lack of input
sanitization can lead to unexpected

00:59:08.037 --> 00:59:09.737
behavior on the part of the application.

00:59:10.367 --> 00:59:14.647
API providers use headers to provide the
consumer with instructions for handling

00:59:14.647 --> 00:59:16.267
the response and security requirements.

00:59:16.877 --> 00:59:19.577
Misconfigured headers can
result in sensitive information

00:59:19.577 --> 00:59:22.967
disclosure, downgrade attacks,
and cross site scripting attacks.

00:59:23.337 --> 00:59:26.537
Many API providers will use
additional services alongside

00:59:26.537 --> 00:59:30.631
their API to enhance API related
metrics or to improve security.

00:59:30.821 --> 00:59:34.251
It's fairly common that those additional
services will add headers to requests

00:59:34.281 --> 00:59:37.521
for metrics and perhaps as some
level of assurance to the consumer.

00:59:38.121 --> 00:59:40.941
The X-Powered-By header
reveals back end technology.

00:59:41.331 --> 00:59:45.511
Headers like this one often advertise the
exact supporting service and its version.

00:59:46.021 --> 00:59:49.271
An attacker could use information
like this to search for exploits

00:59:49.301 --> 00:59:51.191
published for that version of software.

00:59:51.811 --> 00:59:55.341
The X-XSS-Protection header is
exactly what it sounds like, a

00:59:55.341 --> 00:59:57.771
header that's meant to prevent
cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks.

00:59:58.251 --> 01:00:01.091
Cross site scripting is a common
type of injection vulnerability where

01:00:01.091 --> 01:00:04.681
an attacker could insert scripts
into a web page and trick end users

01:00:04.681 --> 01:00:06.421
into clicking on malicious links.

01:00:06.961 --> 01:00:11.781
A X-XSS-Protection header with a
value of 0 indicates no protections

01:00:11.781 --> 01:00:15.561
in place, and a value of 1 indicates
that the protection is turned on.

01:00:15.561 --> 01:00:20.048
This header and others like it,
clearly reveals whether or not

01:00:20.078 --> 01:00:21.318
a security control is in place.

01:00:22.773 --> 01:00:26.603
The X-Response-Time header is middleware
that provides the usage metrics.

01:00:27.053 --> 01:00:30.603
In this example, in the written
course material, its value

01:00:30.603 --> 01:00:33.133
represents 566 milliseconds.

01:00:33.373 --> 01:00:37.833
But if the API isn't configured properly,
this header can function as a side

01:00:37.833 --> 01:00:40.103
channel used to reveal existing resources.

01:00:40.493 --> 01:00:44.463
If the X-Response-Time header has
a consistent response time for non

01:00:44.463 --> 01:00:49.813
existing records, for example, but
increases its response time for certain

01:00:49.843 --> 01:00:53.723
other records that actually exist, this
could be an indication to an attacker

01:00:54.023 --> 01:00:55.763
that they've come across these files.

01:00:56.743 --> 01:01:00.243
Say, for instance, an attacker can
determine that a bogus account like

01:01:00.283 --> 01:01:05.663
/user/account/thisdefinitelydoesnotexist
has an average response

01:01:05.663 --> 01:01:07.853
time of 25 milliseconds.

01:01:07.853 --> 01:01:11.653
The attacker can know that
their existing account is

01:01:11.653 --> 01:01:17.673
/user/account/1021, which receives an
X-Response-Time of 510 milliseconds.

01:01:18.183 --> 01:01:21.493
An attacker could then send requests
brute forcing account numbers

01:01:21.743 --> 01:01:24.543
and review the results and see
which account numbers resulted in

01:01:24.543 --> 01:01:26.483
drastically increased response times.

01:01:27.243 --> 01:01:31.433
Any API providing sensitive information
to consumers should use Transport Layer

01:01:31.433 --> 01:01:36.043
Security to encrypt the data even if
the API is only provided internally,

01:01:36.163 --> 01:01:38.393
privately, or at a partner level.

01:01:38.673 --> 01:01:44.013
Transport Layer Security, the protocol
that encrypts HTTPS traffic, is one of

01:01:44.013 --> 01:01:48.193
the most basic ways to ensure that API
requests and responses are protected

01:01:48.193 --> 01:01:50.233
when being passed across a network.

01:01:50.813 --> 01:01:55.043
Misconfigured or missing transit
encryption can cause API users to pass

01:01:55.073 --> 01:01:59.233
sensitive API information in clear
text across networks, in which case an

01:01:59.233 --> 01:02:02.643
attacker could capture the responses
and requests with a Man-in-the-Middle

01:02:02.643 --> 01:02:04.743
(MITM) and read them plainly.

01:02:05.323 --> 01:02:08.913
They would simply have to  intercept
the network traffic with a network

01:02:08.923 --> 01:02:13.033
protocol analyzer like Wireshark to
see the information being communicated

01:02:13.033 --> 01:02:14.713
between a consumer and a provider.

01:02:15.483 --> 01:02:19.773
When a service uses default accounts or
credentials, and the defaults are known,

01:02:19.773 --> 01:02:23.343
an attacker can use those credentials
to assume the role of that account.

01:02:23.798 --> 01:02:27.568
This could allow them to gain access to
sensitive information or administrative

01:02:27.568 --> 01:02:31.248
functionality and potentially lead to
a compromise of the supporting systems.

01:02:31.248 --> 01:02:36.668
Lastly, if an API provider allows
unnecessary HTTP methods, there's

01:02:36.668 --> 01:02:39.528
an increased risk that the
application won't handle these

01:02:39.528 --> 01:02:43.873
methods properly or will result in
sensitive information disclosure.

01:02:44.483 --> 01:02:51.316
Out of all the vulnerabilities covered
on the OWASP Top 10, API8:2023 Security

01:02:51.316 --> 01:02:54.613
Misconfiguration is one of the only
ones that can be detected by web

01:02:54.613 --> 01:02:56.093
application vulnerability scanners.

01:02:56.443 --> 01:03:01.473
Automated scanners like BurpSuite,
Nessus, Qualys, OWASP, ZAP, and Nikto

01:03:01.613 --> 01:03:05.463
will automatically check responses
from the web server to determine the

01:03:05.463 --> 01:03:09.663
version information, headers, cookies,
transit encryption configuration,

01:03:09.663 --> 01:03:13.343
and parameters to see if expected
security measures are missing.

01:03:14.553 --> 01:03:18.563
Security misconfigurations can also be
checked manually if you know what you're

01:03:18.563 --> 01:03:23.173
looking for, by inspecting the headers,
SSL certificate, cookies, and parameters.

01:03:23.941 --> 01:03:27.941
The OWASP Preventative Measures states
the API lifecycle should include:

01:03:28.011 --> 01:03:31.541
repeatable hardening process leading
to a fast and easy deployment of a

01:03:31.541 --> 01:03:35.871
properly locked out environment, a task
to review and update configurations

01:03:35.871 --> 01:03:37.591
across the entire API stack.

01:03:37.931 --> 01:03:41.461
The review should include
orchestration files, API components

01:03:41.461 --> 01:03:45.591
and cloud services and automated
process to continuously assess the

01:03:45.591 --> 01:03:48.791
effectiveness of the configuration
and settings in all environments.

01:03:49.471 --> 01:03:53.561
Furthermore, ensure that all API
communications from the client to

01:03:53.561 --> 01:03:57.831
the API server And any downstream
upstream components happen over an

01:03:57.831 --> 01:04:01.371
encrypted communication channel,
regardless of whether it is an

01:04:01.481 --> 01:04:03.501
internal or public facing API.

01:04:04.131 --> 01:04:09.341
Be specific about which HTTP
verbs each API can be accessed by.

01:04:09.771 --> 01:04:12.531
All other HTTP verbs should be disabled.

01:04:13.081 --> 01:04:18.291
API's expecting to be accessed from
browser based clients should at

01:04:18.291 --> 01:04:22.421
least implement a proper Cross Origin
Resource Sharing (CORS) policy.

01:04:22.931 --> 01:04:27.531
Include applicable security headers,
restrict incoming content types/data

01:04:27.908 --> 01:04:30.938
formats to those that meet the
business functional requirements.

01:04:31.308 --> 01:04:35.778
Ensure all servers in the HTTP server
chain process incoming requests in a

01:04:35.778 --> 01:04:38.248
uniform manner to avoid desync issues.

01:04:38.698 --> 01:04:43.558
Where applicable, define and enforce all
API response payload schemas, including

01:04:43.578 --> 01:04:48.048
error responses to prevent exception
traces and other valuable information

01:04:48.048 --> 01:04:49.598
from being sent back to an attacker.

01:04:50.328 --> 01:04:53.568
Check out the written course content
for additional resources about

01:04:53.598 --> 01:04:55.348
API security misconfigurations.

01:05:02.693 --> 01:05:07.303
API9:2023, Improper Inventory
Management represents the risks

01:05:07.303 --> 01:05:10.903
involved with exposing non production
and unsupported API versions.

01:05:11.583 --> 01:05:15.043
When this is present, the non production
and unsupported versions of the API are

01:05:15.063 --> 01:05:18.883
often not protected by the same security
rigor as the production versions.

01:05:19.603 --> 01:05:22.343
This makes improper inventory
management a gateway to other

01:05:22.343 --> 01:05:23.833
API security vulnerabilities.

01:05:24.883 --> 01:05:28.898
The OWASP Attack Vector description states
"Threat agents usually get unauthorized

01:05:28.898 --> 01:05:33.068
access through old API versions or
endpoints left running unpatched and

01:05:33.068 --> 01:05:35.708
using weaker security requirements.

01:05:36.548 --> 01:05:39.628
Alternatively, they may get
access to sensitive data through

01:05:39.628 --> 01:05:42.208
a third party with whom there's
no reason to share data with."

01:05:43.068 --> 01:05:46.458
The OWASP Security Weakness
description states "Outdated

01:05:46.468 --> 01:05:50.278
documentation makes it more difficult
to find and or fix vulnerabilities.

01:05:50.898 --> 01:05:54.298
Lack of assets, inventory, and
retirement strategies leads to

01:05:54.298 --> 01:05:57.948
running unpatched systems, resulting
in leakage of sensitive data.

01:05:58.508 --> 01:06:03.208
It's common to find unnecessarily exposed
API hosts because of modern concepts like

01:06:03.228 --> 01:06:07.858
microservices, which makes applications
easy to deploy and independent.

01:06:08.568 --> 01:06:12.208
Simple Google Dorking, DNS
enumeration, or using specialized

01:06:12.208 --> 01:06:16.848
search engines for various types of
servers connected to the internet

01:06:16.848 --> 01:06:18.388
will be enough to discover targets."

01:06:19.143 --> 01:06:23.803
The OWASP Impacts description states
"Attackers can gain access to sensitive

01:06:23.803 --> 01:06:25.733
data or even take over the server.

01:06:26.133 --> 01:06:29.973
Sometimes different API version
deployments are connected to the

01:06:30.053 --> 01:06:31.513
same database with real data.

01:06:32.193 --> 01:06:37.683
Threat agents may exploit deprecated
endpoints available to in old API versions

01:06:37.733 --> 01:06:41.303
to get access to administrative functions
or exploit known vulnerabilities."

01:06:41.810 --> 01:06:46.040
Improper Inventory Management takes place
when an organization exposes APIs that

01:06:46.040 --> 01:06:47.830
are unsupported or still in development.

01:06:48.440 --> 01:06:51.980
As with any software, old API
versions are more likely to contain

01:06:51.980 --> 01:06:54.890
vulnerabilities because they are no
longer being patched and upgraded.

01:06:55.490 --> 01:06:59.680
Likewise, APIs that are still being
developed and are typically not as secure

01:06:59.680 --> 01:07:01.300
as their production API counterparts.

01:07:01.960 --> 01:07:05.220
Improper inventory management can
lead to other vulnerabilities such as

01:07:05.400 --> 01:07:10.100
excessive data exposure, information
disclosure, mass assignment, improper

01:07:10.100 --> 01:07:12.290
rate limiting, and API injection.

01:07:12.805 --> 01:07:16.275
For attackers, this means that discovering
an improper inventory management

01:07:16.275 --> 01:07:20.195
vulnerability is only the first step
toward further exploitation of an API.

01:07:20.825 --> 01:07:24.575
Detecting Improper Inventory Management
can be tested by using outdated

01:07:24.595 --> 01:07:29.375
API documentation, changelogs, and
version history on repositories.

01:07:29.735 --> 01:07:33.565
For example, if an organization's API
documentation has not been updated

01:07:33.885 --> 01:07:37.655
along with the API's endpoints, it
could contain references to portions of

01:07:37.655 --> 01:07:39.825
the API that are no longer supported.

01:07:40.630 --> 01:07:43.560
Organizations often include
versioning information in their

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:46.610
endpoints names to distinguish
between older and newer versions.

01:07:47.080 --> 01:07:50.500
Such as /v1/, /v2/, or /v3/ and so on.

01:07:50.980 --> 01:07:54.640
APIs still in development often
use paths such as /alpha/,

01:07:54.680 --> 01:07:56.990
/beta/, /test/, /uat/ and /demo/.

01:07:57.400 --> 01:08:04.070
If you know that an API is now using
apiv3.org/admin, but a part of the API

01:08:04.070 --> 01:08:09.590
documentation refers to apiv1.org/admin,
you could try testing different endpoints

01:08:09.590 --> 01:08:12.800
to see if apiv1 or apiv2 are still active.

01:08:13.390 --> 01:08:16.630
Additionally, the organization's
change log may disclose the reasons

01:08:16.630 --> 01:08:18.950
why v1 was updated or retired.

01:08:19.570 --> 01:08:23.370
If an attacker has access to v1,
they can test those for weaknesses.

01:08:24.034 --> 01:08:27.354
Outside of using documentation, an
attacker could discover improper

01:08:27.364 --> 01:08:30.584
inventory vulnerabilities through
the use of guessing, fuzzing,

01:08:30.594 --> 01:08:31.814
or brute forcing requests.

01:08:32.524 --> 01:08:36.364
Testing for improper assets management
is all about discovering unsupported

01:08:36.384 --> 01:08:38.344
and non production versions of an API.

01:08:38.964 --> 01:08:42.064
API providers will often update
services and the newer version

01:08:42.064 --> 01:08:48.049
of the API in a variety of ways,
such as api.target.com/v3, or

01:08:48.049 --> 01:08:52.419
/api/v2/accounts, or /api/v3/accounts.

01:08:52.729 --> 01:08:55.639
API versioning could also
be maintained as a header.

01:08:55.879 --> 01:09:01.689
So you could see something like Accept:
version=2.0, or Accept api-version=3.

01:09:02.169 --> 01:09:04.789
In addition, versioning could
also be set within a query

01:09:04.789 --> 01:09:06.479
parameter or the request body.

01:09:06.949 --> 01:09:11.149
So you could see /api/accounts?ver=2.

01:09:11.589 --> 01:09:15.619
Or POST to API accounts with
the versioning equals 1.0.

01:09:15.879 --> 01:09:19.879
Non production versions of an API
include any version of the API that

01:09:19.879 --> 01:09:21.859
was not meant for end user consumption.

01:09:22.319 --> 01:09:28.979
Non production versions could include
api.test.target.com, beta.api.com,

01:09:29.289 --> 01:09:33.679
/api/private, /api/partner or /api/test.

01:09:34.059 --> 01:09:37.169
In these instances, earlier
versions of the API may no longer

01:09:37.169 --> 01:09:41.039
be patched or updated since the
older versions lack this support.

01:09:41.039 --> 01:09:44.749
They may expose the API to additional
vulnerabilities and lead an attacker

01:09:44.749 --> 01:09:47.729
to a path that can be used to
compromise the provider's data.

01:09:47.921 --> 01:09:52.181
The OWASP Preventative Measures states
inventory all API hosts and document

01:09:52.231 --> 01:09:56.781
important aspects of each one of them,
focusing on the API environment, such as

01:09:56.781 --> 01:10:00.971
production, staging, test or development,
who should have network access to the

01:10:00.971 --> 01:10:06.591
host, such as public, internal or partners
and the API version inventory integrated

01:10:06.591 --> 01:10:11.101
services and document important aspects,
such as their role in the system, what

01:10:11.101 --> 01:10:13.391
data is exchanged and its sensitivity.

01:10:14.006 --> 01:10:19.356
Document all aspects of your API, such as
authentication, errors, redirects, rate

01:10:19.356 --> 01:10:24.216
limiting, cross origin resource sharing,
CORS policy, and endpoints, including

01:10:24.216 --> 01:10:26.596
their parameters, requests, and responses.

01:10:27.216 --> 01:10:30.276
Generate documentation automatically
by adopting open standards.

01:10:30.706 --> 01:10:33.566
Include the documentation
built in your CI/CD pipeline.

01:10:34.126 --> 01:10:37.596
Make API documentation available
to those authorized to use the API.

01:10:38.626 --> 01:10:42.256
Use external protection measures,
such as an API security firewall, for

01:10:42.256 --> 01:10:44.056
all exposed versions of your APIs.

01:10:44.146 --> 01:10:45.876
Not just for the current
production versions.

01:10:46.761 --> 01:10:50.261
Avoid using production data with
non production API deployments.

01:10:50.431 --> 01:10:53.841
If this is unavoidable, these
endpoints should get the same security

01:10:53.841 --> 01:10:55.291
treatment as the production ones.

01:10:55.801 --> 01:10:59.541
When newer versions of the API include
security improvements, perform risks

01:10:59.541 --> 01:11:03.721
analysis, risk analysis to make the
decision of the mitigation actions

01:11:03.721 --> 01:11:05.081
required for the older version.

01:11:05.101 --> 01:11:09.371
For example, whether it is possible to
backport the improvements without breaking

01:11:09.401 --> 01:11:14.281
API compatibility, or you need to take
the older version, but quickly enforce

01:11:14.291 --> 01:11:16.501
all clients to move to the latest version.

01:11:17.161 --> 01:11:20.511
For additional resources about
improper inventory management, check

01:11:20.521 --> 01:11:21.801
out the written course materials.

01:11:25.018 --> 01:11:29.918
API10:2023, Unsafe Consumption of
APIs is the only item on the Top 10

01:11:29.918 --> 01:11:33.398
list that focuses less on the risks
of being an API provider and more on

01:11:33.398 --> 01:11:35.168
the risks of being an API consumer.

01:11:35.918 --> 01:11:37.908
Unsafe consumption is
really a trust issue.

01:11:37.918 --> 01:11:41.878
When an application is consuming the data
of a third party API, it should treat

01:11:41.878 --> 01:11:43.718
those with a similar trust to user input.

01:11:44.428 --> 01:11:46.768
By that, I mean there should
be little to no trust.

01:11:47.233 --> 01:11:50.983
So data consumed from third party API
should be treated with similar security

01:11:50.983 --> 01:11:53.233
standards as end user supplied input.

01:11:53.823 --> 01:11:57.353
If a third party API provider is
compromised, then that insecure

01:11:57.373 --> 01:12:00.603
API connection back to the consumer
becomes a new attack vector

01:12:00.603 --> 01:12:01.913
for the attacker to leverage.

01:12:02.413 --> 01:12:05.563
In the case of an insecure API
connection, that could mean the complete

01:12:05.563 --> 01:12:10.343
compromise of organizations insecurely
consuming data from that API provider.

01:12:11.698 --> 01:12:15.838
The OWASP Attack Vector description
states "exploiting this issue requires

01:12:15.838 --> 01:12:19.998
attackers to identify and potentially
compromise other APIs or services

01:12:20.358 --> 01:12:21.988
the target API is integrated with.

01:12:22.528 --> 01:12:26.698
Usually this information is not publicly
available or the integrated API or

01:12:26.698 --> 01:12:28.508
service is not easily exploitable."

01:12:29.423 --> 01:12:34.123
The OWASP Security Weakness description
states "developers tend to trust and not

01:12:34.123 --> 01:12:38.603
verify the endpoints that interact with
external or third party APIs relying

01:12:38.603 --> 01:12:42.353
on weaker security requirements such
as those regarding transport security,

01:12:42.833 --> 01:12:46.363
authentication, authorization, and
input validation and sanitization.

01:12:47.623 --> 01:12:52.143
Attackers need to identify services the
target API interacts with or integrates

01:12:52.143 --> 01:12:54.373
with and eventually compromise them."

01:12:54.928 --> 01:12:59.298
The OWASP Impacts description states
"the impact varies according to what

01:12:59.298 --> 01:13:01.578
the target API does with pooled data.

01:13:02.018 --> 01:13:05.528
Successful exploitation may lead to
sensitive information exposure to

01:13:05.528 --> 01:13:09.278
unauthorized actors, many kinds of
injections, or denial of service."

01:13:10.470 --> 01:13:15.250
Most of the 2023 API Security Top 10
is about APIs and the API provider.

01:13:15.750 --> 01:13:19.190
An API can often serve as the path
of least resistance for an attacker.

01:13:19.600 --> 01:13:23.310
So if an attacker compromises a third
party API provider, then that third

01:13:23.310 --> 01:13:26.790
party's connection to other businesses
can become an additional attack vector.

01:13:27.340 --> 01:13:31.110
If that API is over an unencrypted
connection, then an attacker would be able

01:13:31.110 --> 01:13:32.910
to capture sensitive data in clear text.

01:13:33.390 --> 01:13:36.980
If that third party API isn't held
to similar security standards as

01:13:37.000 --> 01:13:40.170
internet facing APIs, then it could
also be vulnerable to injection,

01:13:41.855 --> 01:13:44.165
authorization, authentication
and other compromising attacks.

01:13:44.815 --> 01:13:48.375
The OWASP Preventative Measure states
when evaluating service providers

01:13:48.425 --> 01:13:50.335
assess their API security posture.

01:13:50.775 --> 01:13:54.715
Ensure all API interactions happen
over a secure communication channel.

01:13:55.395 --> 01:13:58.295
Always validate and properly
sanitize data received from

01:13:58.305 --> 01:14:00.245
integrated APIs before using it.

01:14:00.835 --> 01:14:04.325
Maintain and allow a list of
well known locations integrated

01:14:04.345 --> 01:14:06.365
APIs may redirect yours to.

01:14:06.505 --> 01:14:08.575
Do not blindly follow redirects.

01:14:09.335 --> 01:14:13.250
For additional Resources about
unsafe consumption of APIs, check

01:14:13.250 --> 01:14:14.490
out the written course materials.

01:14:21.948 --> 01:14:25.368
Injection vulnerabilities have plagued
web applications for over two decades.

01:14:25.928 --> 01:14:29.269
They take place when an attacker can
send commands that are executed by the

01:14:29.269 --> 01:14:31.129
systems that support the web application.

01:14:31.659 --> 01:14:35.459
The most common forms of injection attacks
are SQL injection, cross site scripting,

01:14:35.459 --> 01:14:37.249
and operating system command injection.

01:14:37.819 --> 01:14:41.919
APIs are yet another attack vector for
these critical attacks to be communicated

01:14:41.919 --> 01:14:46.009
from an attacker to the supporting
system, supporting databases and systems.

01:14:46.879 --> 01:14:52.089
The OWASP 2019 Attack Vector description
states, "attackers will feed the API

01:14:52.109 --> 01:14:55.359
with malicious data through whatever
injection vectors are available,

01:14:55.429 --> 01:14:59.399
such as direct input parameters,
integrated services, expecting

01:14:59.399 --> 01:15:01.026
it to be sent to an interpreter."

01:15:01.526 --> 01:15:05.966
The OWASP 2019 Security Weakness
description states, "injection flaws

01:15:05.966 --> 01:15:10.436
are very common and are often found
in SQL, LDAP, or NoSQL queries,

01:15:10.706 --> 01:15:13.406
OS commands, XML parsers, and ORM.

01:15:13.901 --> 01:15:16.951
These flaws are easy to discover
when reviewing the source code.

01:15:17.371 --> 01:15:20.081
Attackers can use scanners
and fuzzers to find these."

01:15:21.171 --> 01:15:25.341
The OWASP 2019 Impacts Description
states, Injection can lead to

01:15:25.341 --> 01:15:28.601
information disclosure and data
loss and may also lead to denial of

01:15:28.601 --> 01:15:30.531
service or complete host takeover.

01:15:31.273 --> 01:15:34.203
Injection flaws exist when a
request is passed to the API

01:15:34.203 --> 01:15:37.143
supporting infrastructure and the
API provider doesn't filter the

01:15:37.143 --> 01:15:39.273
input to remove unwanted characters.

01:15:39.753 --> 01:15:42.273
As a result, the infrastructure
might treat the data from the

01:15:42.293 --> 01:15:43.973
request as code and run it.

01:15:44.433 --> 01:15:47.813
When this sort of flaw is present, an
attacker will be able to conduct injection

01:15:47.813 --> 01:15:51.753
attacks like SQL injection, NoSQL
injection, and system command injection.

01:15:52.243 --> 01:15:55.373
When these injection attacks are
successful, the API delivers an

01:15:55.383 --> 01:15:58.453
unsanitized payload directly to
the operating system running the

01:15:58.453 --> 01:16:00.993
application or a query to its database.

01:16:01.883 --> 01:16:05.193
As a result, if an attacker sends
a payload containing SQL commands

01:16:05.193 --> 01:16:09.513
to a vulnerable API that uses a
SQL database, the API will pass the

01:16:09.513 --> 01:16:13.163
commands to the database, which will
process and perform the commands.

01:16:13.723 --> 01:16:17.343
The same will happen with vulnerable
NoSQL databases and affected systems.

01:16:18.373 --> 01:16:22.973
Verbose error messaging, HTTP response
codes, and unexpected API behavior

01:16:22.983 --> 01:16:26.293
can all be clues to an attacker
and will be an indication that they

01:16:26.293 --> 01:16:27.773
have discovered an injection flaw.

01:16:28.363 --> 01:16:33.993
Say, for example, an attacker were
to send OR1 00 DAS as an address

01:16:34.013 --> 01:16:35.653
in an account registration process.

01:16:36.153 --> 01:16:40.173
The API may pass that payload
directly back to the back end SQL

01:16:40.193 --> 01:16:44.603
database, where the or 1 equals 0
statement, which would fail as 1 does

01:16:44.603 --> 01:16:46.973
not equal 0, causing a SQL error.

01:16:47.673 --> 01:16:51.633
An error in the backend database could
show up as a response to the consumer.

01:16:52.113 --> 01:16:55.473
In this case, the attacker, who's
the consumer, might receive a

01:16:55.473 --> 01:16:59.696
response like, Error equals you
have an error in your SQL syntax.

01:17:00.226 --> 01:17:04.116
But, any response directly from the
database or the supporting system will

01:17:04.116 --> 01:17:07.606
serve as a clear indicator that there's
likely an injection vulnerability.

01:17:08.356 --> 01:17:11.716
Injection vulnerabilities are often
complemented by other weaknesses

01:17:11.716 --> 01:17:13.336
like poor input sanitization.

01:17:14.016 --> 01:17:17.276
Injection flaws can have serious
impacts by providing an attacker

01:17:17.276 --> 01:17:20.496
with the ability to manipulate an
API supporting system or database.

01:17:21.211 --> 01:17:24.851
Finding injection flaws requires
diligently testing API endpoints and

01:17:24.851 --> 01:17:28.891
paying attention to how the API responds,
then crafting requests that attempt

01:17:28.901 --> 01:17:30.291
to manipulate the backend systems.

01:17:30.991 --> 01:17:34.941
Injection attacks have been around for
decades, so there are many standard

01:17:34.941 --> 01:17:38.111
security controls that can be used
to protect API providers from them.

01:17:38.384 --> 01:17:42.064
The OWASP 2019 Preventative
Measures states, Preventing

01:17:42.064 --> 01:17:45.504
injection requires keeping data
separate from commands and queries.

01:17:45.944 --> 01:17:48.724
Perform data validation using
a single trustworthy and

01:17:48.734 --> 01:17:50.354
actively maintained library.

01:17:50.804 --> 01:17:54.934
Validate, filter, and sanitize all
client provided data or other data

01:17:54.934 --> 01:17:56.344
coming from integrated systems.

01:17:57.314 --> 01:18:00.494
Special characters should be
escaped using the specific syntax

01:18:00.514 --> 01:18:01.774
for the target interpreter.

01:18:02.404 --> 01:18:05.554
Prefer a safe API that provides
parameterized interface.

01:18:06.304 --> 01:18:09.004
Always limit the number of
returned records to prevent mass

01:18:09.004 --> 01:18:10.574
disclosure in case of injection.

01:18:11.394 --> 01:18:14.684
Validate incoming data using
sufficient filters to only allow

01:18:14.714 --> 01:18:17.044
valid values for each input parameter.

01:18:17.684 --> 01:18:21.144
Define data types and strict
patterns for all string parameters.

01:18:21.874 --> 01:18:24.564
For additional resources
about API injection, check out

01:18:24.564 --> 01:18:25.644
the written course content.

01:18:27.926 --> 01:18:32.166
Logging and Monitoring are a necessary
and important layer of API security.

01:18:32.516 --> 01:18:36.113
In order to detect an attack
against an API, an organization

01:18:36.123 --> 01:18:37.683
must have monitoring in place.

01:18:38.163 --> 01:18:41.923
Without sufficient logging and monitoring,
an API provider is operating in the

01:18:41.923 --> 01:18:45.993
dark, and API attacks are guaranteed to
go unnoticed until it's far too late.

01:18:46.823 --> 01:18:51.533
The OWASP 2019 Attack Vector description
states, "Attackers take advantage of

01:18:51.533 --> 01:18:54.773
a lack of logging and monitoring to
abuse systems without being noticed."

01:18:55.498 --> 01:18:59.478
The OWASP 2019 Security Weakness
description states, "Without logging

01:18:59.478 --> 01:19:03.741
and monitoring, or insufficient logging
and monitoring, it is almost impossible

01:19:03.741 --> 01:19:07.411
to track suspicious activities and
respond to them in a timely fashion."

01:19:08.171 --> 01:19:13.281
The OWASP 2019 Impacts description states,
"Without visibility over ongoing malicious

01:19:13.281 --> 01:19:16.851
activities, attackers have plenty
of time to fully compromise systems.

01:19:17.976 --> 01:19:21.966
Logs can reveal patterns in API
usage and can be used as evidence

01:19:21.966 --> 01:19:24.016
to understand how an API is abused.

01:19:24.336 --> 01:19:27.976
Logging and monitoring provide an
audit trail of activities and are often

01:19:27.976 --> 01:19:29.726
required for compliance purposes."

01:19:30.156 --> 01:19:33.536
An important part of logging is to
ensure that the logs have integrity

01:19:33.566 --> 01:19:35.266
and cannot be altered by an attacker.

01:19:35.796 --> 01:19:39.636
Monitoring an API will help providers
detect suspicious activities

01:19:39.656 --> 01:19:41.236
and anomalous user behavior.

01:19:41.846 --> 01:19:45.086
This helps providers be able to
take action to thwart attacks.

01:19:45.596 --> 01:19:49.705
Logging and monitoring are essential for
improving API performance and security.

01:19:49.855 --> 01:19:54.145
The OWASP 2019 Preventative
Measures states, log all failed

01:19:54.145 --> 01:19:58.495
authentication attempts, denied
access, and input validation errors.

01:19:59.070 --> 01:20:03.444
Logs should be written using a format
suited to be consumed by a log management

01:20:03.454 --> 01:20:07.214
solution and should include enough
detail to identify the malicious actor.

01:20:07.864 --> 01:20:10.924
Logs should be handled as sensitive
data and their integrity should

01:20:10.924 --> 01:20:13.204
be guaranteed at rest and transit.

01:20:13.804 --> 01:20:17.374
Configure a monitoring system to
continuously monitor the infrastructure

01:20:17.374 --> 01:20:18.824
network and API functioning.

01:20:19.414 --> 01:20:23.744
Use a SIEM system to aggregate and
manage logs from all components

01:20:23.764 --> 01:20:25.568
of the API stack and hosts.

01:20:26.069 --> 01:20:30.302
Configure custom dashboards and alerts
enabling suspicious activities to be

01:20:30.302 --> 01:20:32.402
detected and responded to earlier.

01:20:33.202 --> 01:20:36.412
For additional resources about
insufficient logging and monitoring,

01:20:36.422 --> 01:20:37.902
check out the written course content.

01:20:40.794 --> 01:20:43.824
Business logic vulnerabilities
are weaknesses within applications

01:20:43.824 --> 01:20:47.014
that are unique to the policies and
features of a given API provider.

01:20:47.574 --> 01:20:50.984
The exploitation of business logic
takes place when an attacker leverages

01:20:51.014 --> 01:20:54.034
misplaced trust or features of
an application against the API.

01:20:54.754 --> 01:20:57.544
Identifying business logic
vulnerabilities can be challenging due

01:20:57.544 --> 01:20:59.394
to the unique nature of each business.

01:20:59.844 --> 01:21:03.284
The impact of these vulnerabilities
can range based on the severity of

01:21:03.304 --> 01:21:04.964
the vulnerable policy or feature.

01:21:05.824 --> 01:21:09.969
My attack vector description for this
Business logic vulnerabilities are

01:21:09.969 --> 01:21:13.679
unique to each application and exploit
the normal intended functioning of

01:21:13.679 --> 01:21:15.109
an application's business processes.

01:21:15.944 --> 01:21:19.284
They often require specific knowledge
of the application's functionality

01:21:19.304 --> 01:21:21.244
and the flow of transactions or data.

01:21:21.844 --> 01:21:24.614
Since these vulnerabilities are
specific to the business logic of

01:21:24.654 --> 01:21:28.614
each application, there's no one size
fits all approach to identifying them.

01:21:29.474 --> 01:21:34.264
My security weakness description states,
business logic vulnerabilities arise when

01:21:34.264 --> 01:21:38.504
the assumptions and constraints of a given
business process aren't properly enforced

01:21:38.524 --> 01:21:40.894
in the application's control structures.

01:21:41.469 --> 01:21:44.399
This allows users to manipulate
the application's functionality

01:21:44.399 --> 01:21:47.029
to achieve outcomes that are
detrimental to the business.

01:21:47.709 --> 01:21:52.299
These weaknesses typically occur when
developers fail to anticipate the various

01:21:52.299 --> 01:21:56.809
ways that an application's features can
be misused or when they don't consider

01:21:56.809 --> 01:21:58.869
the wider context of the business rules.

01:21:59.489 --> 01:22:02.679
This is often due to a lack of
comprehensive understanding of the

01:22:02.679 --> 01:22:07.209
application's business logic, a lack
of input validation, or incomplete

01:22:07.259 --> 01:22:09.029
function level authorization checks.

01:22:09.849 --> 01:22:14.454
My impacts description states Business
logic vulnerabilities can cause a variety

01:22:14.454 --> 01:22:18.374
of technical impacts depending on the
specific flaw in the systems involved.

01:22:18.974 --> 01:22:23.334
These impacts can range from unauthorized
access to data or functionality to

01:22:23.334 --> 01:22:25.434
a total bypass of system controls.

01:22:26.000 --> 01:22:29.870
Business logic vulnerabilities, also
known as business logic flaws, or BLFs,

01:22:30.310 --> 01:22:33.880
are intended features of an application
that attackers can use maliciously.

01:22:34.350 --> 01:22:38.130
For example, if an API has an upload
feature that instructs users to only

01:22:38.140 --> 01:22:41.290
upload certain encoded payloads,
but doesn't actually validate those

01:22:41.290 --> 01:22:45.460
encoded payloads, a user could upload
any file as long as it was encoded.

01:22:46.110 --> 01:22:49.700
This would allow end users to upload
and potentially execute arbitrary

01:22:49.760 --> 01:22:51.510
code, including malicious payloads.

01:22:52.135 --> 01:22:55.095
Vulnerabilities of this sort normally
come about from an assumption

01:22:55.095 --> 01:22:58.885
that API consumers will follow
directions, be trustworthy, or

01:22:58.905 --> 01:23:00.735
only use the API in a certain way.

01:23:01.235 --> 01:23:04.665
In those cases, the organization
essentially depends on trust as

01:23:04.665 --> 01:23:07.975
a security control by expecting
the consumer to act benevolently.

01:23:08.655 --> 01:23:12.685
Unfortunately, even good natured API
consumers make mistakes that could lead

01:23:12.685 --> 01:23:14.425
to a compromise of the application.

01:23:14.965 --> 01:23:20.095
The Experian partner API leak in 2021 was
a great example of an API trust failure.

01:23:20.715 --> 01:23:24.985
A certain Experian partner was authorized
to use Experian's API to perform credit

01:23:24.985 --> 01:23:28.835
checks, but the partner added the API's
credit check functionality to their web

01:23:28.835 --> 01:23:32.935
application and inadvertently exposed
all partner level requests to users.

01:23:33.595 --> 01:23:36.505
This request could be intercepted
when using the partner's web

01:23:36.505 --> 01:23:40.745
application, and if it included a
name and address, the Experian API

01:23:40.755 --> 01:23:44.205
would respond with the individual's
credit score and credit risk factors.

01:23:44.850 --> 01:23:48.930
One of the leading causes of this business
logic vulnerability was that the Experian

01:23:48.940 --> 01:23:51.910
trusted the partner to not expose the API.

01:23:53.230 --> 01:23:55.820
Another problem with trust
is that credentials like API

01:23:55.820 --> 01:23:59.040
keys, tokens, and passwords are
constantly being stolen and leaked.

01:23:59.400 --> 01:24:02.700
When a trusted consumer's credentials
are stolen, the consumer can become a

01:24:02.700 --> 01:24:04.990
wolf in sheep's clothing and wreak havoc.

01:24:05.540 --> 01:24:09.090
Without strong technical controls in
place, business logic vulnerabilities can

01:24:09.110 --> 01:24:12.720
often have the most significant impact
leading to exploitation and compromise.

01:24:13.376 --> 01:24:17.566
An API's documentation can be a telltale
sign of a business logic vulnerability.

01:24:18.046 --> 01:24:20.566
Statements like the following
should be indications of

01:24:20.566 --> 01:24:21.966
potential business logic flaws.

01:24:22.676 --> 01:24:25.636
Only use feature x to perform function y.

01:24:26.136 --> 01:24:28.376
Do not do x with endpoint y.

01:24:28.811 --> 01:24:31.321
Only admins should perform request X.

01:24:31.831 --> 01:24:35.261
These statements may indicate
that the API provider is trusting

01:24:35.271 --> 01:24:39.761
that end users won't do any of the
discouraged actions as instructed.

01:24:40.241 --> 01:24:43.641
An attacker will easily disobey such
requests to test for the presence

01:24:43.641 --> 01:24:45.361
of technical security controls.

01:24:45.961 --> 01:24:50.041
Another business logic vulnerability
comes about when developers assume

01:24:50.041 --> 01:24:54.571
that consumers will exclusively use
a browser to interact with the web

01:24:54.571 --> 01:24:58.171
application and won't capture API requests
that take place behind the scenes.

01:24:58.936 --> 01:25:02.256
All it takes to exploit this sort of
weakness is for an attacker to intercept

01:25:02.256 --> 01:25:05.546
requests and alter the API request
before it is sent to the provider.

01:25:06.156 --> 01:25:09.366
This would allow the attacker to
capture shared API keys or use

01:25:09.366 --> 01:25:12.456
parameters that could negatively
impact the security of the application.

01:25:13.156 --> 01:25:16.466
As an example, consider a web
app authentication portal that

01:25:16.516 --> 01:25:19.676
a user normally would employ to
authenticate to their account.

01:25:20.426 --> 01:25:24.196
Say the web application issued an API
authentication request that included the

01:25:24.196 --> 01:25:28.676
username and password along with a query
parameter, multi factor equals true.

01:25:29.246 --> 01:25:32.686
There's a chance that an attacker could
bypass the multi factor authentication by

01:25:32.686 --> 01:25:34.866
simply altering the parameter to false.

01:25:35.586 --> 01:25:37.916
Testing for business logic
flaws can be challenging

01:25:37.916 --> 01:25:39.576
because each business is unique.

01:25:40.071 --> 01:25:43.471
Automated scanners will have a difficult
time detecting these issues, as the

01:25:43.471 --> 01:25:45.671
flaws are part of the API's intended use.

01:25:46.261 --> 01:25:49.531
You must understand how the
business and API operate, and then

01:25:49.531 --> 01:25:52.681
consider how an attacker could use
these features to their advantage.

01:25:52.971 --> 01:25:55.831
One method of testing for business
logic flaws is to study the

01:25:55.851 --> 01:25:59.801
application's business logic with an
adversarial mindset and try breaking

01:25:59.821 --> 01:26:01.231
any assumptions that have been made.

01:26:01.837 --> 01:26:05.207
My preventative measures for business
logic flaws include the following.

01:26:05.657 --> 01:26:07.257
Use a threat modeling approach.

01:26:07.507 --> 01:26:10.867
Understand the business processes
and workflows your API supports.

01:26:11.467 --> 01:26:15.027
Identify the potential threats,
weaknesses, and risks during the design

01:26:15.027 --> 01:26:18.777
phase that can help to uncover and
mitigate business logic vulnerabilities.

01:26:19.717 --> 01:26:23.387
Reduce or remove trust relationships
with users, systems, or components.

01:26:24.162 --> 01:26:27.032
Business logic vulnerabilities
can be used to exploit these trust

01:26:27.032 --> 01:26:29.082
relationships leading to a broader impact.

01:26:29.862 --> 01:26:32.762
Regular training can help
developers to understand and avoid

01:26:32.762 --> 01:26:34.022
business logic vulnerabilities.

01:26:34.312 --> 01:26:37.482
Training should cover secure coding
practices, common vulnerabilities,

01:26:37.522 --> 01:26:40.792
and how to identify potential issues
during design and coding phases.

01:26:41.492 --> 01:26:44.722
Implement a bug bounty program,
third party penetration testing,

01:26:44.942 --> 01:26:46.812
or responsible disclosure policy.

01:26:47.122 --> 01:26:50.882
This allows security researchers who
are a step removed from the design and

01:26:50.882 --> 01:26:54.462
delivery of an application to disclose
vulnerabilities they discover in APIs.

01:26:55.272 --> 01:26:58.442
For additional resources about
business logic vulnerabilities, check

01:26:58.442 --> 01:26:59.692
out the written course material.

01:27:02.921 --> 01:27:06.851
And that wraps up the OWASP API
security top 10 and beyond course.

